Well there is xpdf and ghostscript.  Now whether they handle the latest version of the
PDF format, I'm not sure.  Also, they don't present a document quite as nicely as the
real thing.  Another "universal" product that ignores the OpenVMS workstation base.

The need for something like PDF is real - HTML just doesn't cover all the bases yet.
Also, depending upon what the original document was created with, an automatic HTML
conversion that looks decent sometimes is not available.  I think periodically e-mailing
Adobe and posing the following question may (or may not) help the situation under
OpenVMS:

  Our understanding is that PDF is being promoted as the portable document format of
  the future.  Software to read this format is freely availabe under many major
  software platforms, however one platform integral to our infrastructure is for
  some reason excluded - OpenVMS.  This is an X/Motif GUI, and generally software
  written for the various Unix platforms is not at all difficult to run under the
  OpenVMS operating system.  Without similar availability of the Acrobat Reader
  software for this operating system, we and other shops will continue to seek
  other solutions for our documentation needs.  While the OpenVMS operating system
  represents a small share of workstation environments in many shops, the need
  to produce document formats compatible with OpenVMS will affect production methods
  for years to come.
  
I could go into many specific details, but you get the idea.  I know the situation is
frustrating, but reminding Adobe of the facts couldn't hurt.

James



