From: Bob Salita <softwork@Jupiter.Mcs.Net>
Subject: Cross-platform buying and configuring
Date: 1997/09/30
Message-ID: <60psi3$p4c$1@Nntp1.mcs.net>
X-Deja-AN: 276584937
Followup-To: misc.forsale.computers.workstation
Organization: Softworks Limited
Newsgroups: misc.forsale.computers.workstation,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.hp.hardware,comp.sys.sun.hardware,comp.unix.aix


I'm documenting my experience of selecting, buying, configuring 
and integrating four UNIX systems; a Dec Alpha, HP 9000,
IBM RS/6000 and Sun Sparc. This was needed to develop our
cross-platform software product. I wish I had this information
before I started the process, so I'm entering my experience
into the USENET archives in the hope that it will be useful
to others. Please drop me an email if you have comments.

Here are some highlights from this document:
1. Which workstations I purchased and why. This information
   will quickly become irrelevant as the market changes.
2. Which vendors were selected. All are recommended.
3. How I was able to get all systems to use VGA, a PC
   keyboard and either a serial or PS/2 mouse.
4. How I was able to configure an electronic switch box to
   switch between any of nine different systems.
5. How I split the monitor, keyboard and mouse into two
   locations. Sort of a "Y" cable problem.
6. Various problems, solutions and issues encountered.

To better support all of the popular UNIX platforms, I needed
to buy Dec Alpha, HP 9000 and SUN workstations.
Nothing fancy, just some used workstations would be
adequate. I'm documenting the process that I had to go through
from initial researching, selecting, installing and problem solving.

I broke the task down into three phases. First, is learning the product
lines of each of the three vendors and selecting appropriate models to
purchase. Next finding vendors of used equipment, researching pricing
and making the purchase. Lastly, installing the equipment.

Ideally, the hardware setup should be:
1. All computers on same network. Including the three new
workstations, plus my existing IBM AIX, SCO UNIX, Linux,
Windows 95, NT systems, Netware server, two ISDN routers
and 1 print server.
2. All computers accessible from one VGA monitor, one keyboard
and one mouse. The single access point must be in either
of two locations. This requires electronic switch box(es).
3. All Windows and UNIX systems must be accessible from the
internet.

It took me about two days to learn a vendor's product line,
to research used equipment dealers, and become familiar with
used equipment pricing. This process had to be repeated for each
of the three platforms. The following USENET newsgroups were
enormously useful:

        misc.forsale.computers.workstation
        comp.sys.dec.*
        comp.sys.hp.*
        comp.sys.sun.*

The Dec Alpha was purchased as a new computer. Costing only $1300,
it was the best bargin of the three:
        AS 200 4/166MHZ
        Digital UNIX 4.0a 1-2 users on CD-ROM
        32MB memory
        3.5" floppy
        1GB SCSI-2 disk
        4x CD-ROM
        10BaseT ethernet
        Sound card
        2MB PCI graphics
        Keyboard and mouse
        Two year warranty

The Dec vendor is:
        DCG Computers Inc.
        4 Sandborn Rd., Suite #2
        Londonderry, NH   03053
        603-421-1800
        www.dcginc.com

There are quite a few models of Sun computers. I purchased a used
Sparcstation 10 with a souped-up 3rd party CPU upgrade for $2100.
I chose the Sparc 10 system because it is more upgradable. Other
available models are the Sparc 5, which is not so upgradable.
The Sparc 20, which replaces the Sparc 10, was more expensive
and didn't offer much more. The Sparc 10 specs:

        Sparcstation 10 with power supply and motherboard
        Solaris 2.5.1 installed (had to buy used for $100)
        Ross 110MHz single CPU with 1MB cache
        32MB memory
        CG3 color frame buffer display card
        Type 4 keyboard and optical mouse
        Two 420MB SCSI disks (wow!)
        no CD-ROM (used my Plextor SCSI CD-ROM instead)

Standard on Sparc 10 systems are:
        3.5" floppy
        10BaseT ethernet
        ISDN
        Sound

The Sun vendor is Data Instruments. Ask for Fred Dikeman.
We got to know each other pretty well. Fred was good to work
with:

        Data Instruments, Inc.
        869 Pickens Industrial Dr.
        Suite 16
        Marietta, GA   30062
        770-919-2400
        www.unixworkstation.com

I bought a used HP 9000 model 712 80MHz. The 735 99MH is faster and
somewhat more expensive. I selected the 712 because it uses VGA,
PS/2 keyboard and PS/2 mouse, the 735 has proprietary stuff. The 712
cost $1300 and had these specs:

        HP 9000 712/80 (80MHz CPU)
        HP/UX 10.2 installed (got free from HP)
        32MB memory
        no floppy
        no disk
        no CD-ROM (used my Plextor SCSI CD-ROM instead)
        10BaseT ethernet
        VGA grapics
        Keyboard (uses PS/2 keyboard)
        no mouse (uses PS/2 mouse)

The HP was ordered from Fred, the Sun dealer shown above. Since
Fred ran out of 712 stock, he arranged for another dealer to send me
a 712. Fred's dealer buddy, Scott Kelley, moves lots of HP stuff
and was easy to work with too:

        Cypress Technology, Inc.
        12000 Automobile Blvd.
        Suite B
        Clearwater, FL   33762
        813-557-0911
        www.cypress-tech.com

Installing and configuring the systems was a real pain. There
were bugs, issues, mistakes, broken hardware and grief. Keep in
mind that I required that all systems be compatible with my VGA
multisync monitors, PS/2 keyboard and PS/2 mouse. I'm listing
the problems by system.

Dec Alpha problems:
1. Vendor didn't send the OS on CD-ROM. They were very responsive
when brought to their attention.
2. Somehow the ethernet port got reset from the 10BaseT to AUI
connector. Suddenly all network connectivity was lost. It took
hours to figure out the problem. Once discovered, the documentation
described how to set the "bios" to enable the desired ethernet port.
3. Dec doesn't bundle in a C compiler development system.
I'll give gcc a try.

Sun problems:
1. Memory boards were knocked loose during shipment. The system
seemed to be alive but the monitor didn't get a signal. This was
very confusing because it seemed that the monitor was at fault.
Finally, I opened the box and found the loose memory boards.
Compounding the problem was the unavailability of a standard
Sun monitor. Instead I was using a VGA monitor and 13W3-to-VGA
adapter gizmo.
2. Sun uses a 13W3 connector for video out. I had to buy a
13W3-to-VGA adapter to allow the Sun to use my VGA monitor.
To my delight, the adapter gizmo worked perfectly on my VGA
multisync monitors (CTX 1765GMe and a Panasonic S17).
3. Sun uses a proprietary "Y" cable to split the DB25 into two ports.
A standard DB25 only gets you the first port.
4. Sun uses a non-standard SCSI CD-ROM blocksize. Only Sun CD-ROM
drives come ready to work. However some CD-ROM drives can be made to
use the special blocksize. I had a SCSI Plextor CD-ROM drive sitting
on the shelf unused. When I jumpered the pins labeled "block", it
made the drive compatible with the Sun. I set the Plextor to SCSI
ID 6. As a bonus, it also worked on the HP 712 using the Sun's
settings. There is a Sun CD-ROM FAQ available on the Internet.
5. System was not delievered with a CD-ROM copy an O/S.
Solaris 2.5.1 is available "used" for around $100.
6. Sun doesn't bundle in a C compiler development system.
I'll give gcc a try.

HP problems:
1. System was not delievered with a CD-ROM copy of an O/S.
Luckily, HP is giving away free copies of HP/UX 10.2 at
http://www.software.hp.com/OS_transition.
2. The system booted once then wouldn't boot again.
It looped on a message "Start NFS client subsystem". I eventually
figured out that I had specified an invalid gateway IP number.
A bug in the O/S caused the system to loop during boot-up.
I had to reinstall the O/S but didn't have a CD-ROM of the O/S.
Luckily the free 10.2 disk came in the mail that day.
3. The HP defaults to VGA 1200x1000 66Hz(?). My Panasonic S17
couldn't handle the frequency. Luckily the CTX 1765GMe could and
thus I could see the "bios" and change the frequency to 60Hz which
the Panasonic could handle.
4. The system didn't come with a CD-ROM drive. Luckily my SCSI
Plextor drive, as configured for the Sun, works on the HP too.
5. After several days of use, the disk drive stopped being
recognized as a boot device. I had to reload the O/S again.
This was a one-time problem, it hasn't occured again.
6. HP doesn't bundle in a C compiler development system. Although
there is a partial C compiler provided. It may be enough for my
needs. If not, I'll give gcc a try.

I really wanted to be able to control all my computer systems from
a single console/keyboard/mouse control center at one of two.
locations. These needs influenced which system were
purchased, which gizmos and cables were needed, and how the systems
were configured. My instincts were correct, it is do-able, in fact,
I did it! To my surprise, there is a dearth of knowledge about how
this could be done. Vendors and USENET were of little help.
In fact, they were often wrong.

I glued all the systems together using Belkin's OmniView
monitor/keyboard/mouse electronic switch boxes. I bought three
switch boxes. By using the "cascade feature", I'm able to control
all the systems from one multisync monitor, keyboard, mouse.

Well, actually two mice. One mouse for systems using a DB9 serial
mouse, and a PS/2 mouse for the PS/2 mice systems. Good enough though.
I learned that a serial mouse is not compatible with a PS/2 mouse.
They use different voltages. It isn't possible to create a simple
adapter to merely change signal lines. It is more complicated then
that. Anyone know of an electronic gizmo that will make the two
compatible? The DB9 to PS/2 adapters for combo mice do not apply here.

Here is the configuration of the switch boxes (using cascade mode):

Top Tier - OmniView 4 port PS/2 model
Console: Goes to "Y" splitters, then onto monitor, keyboard and
        serial and PS/2 mice. The "Y" splitters clones the
        signal so that either of two locations can have access to
        the systems. Much nicer than a mechanical switch box.
	You can split the monitor using a "Y" cable but you can't
	split the mouse or keyboard with a cable. You need an
	active electronic device. I recommend using an electronic
	splitter for the monitor too because it boosts the signal.
Port 1: Windows 95 system
Port 2: Windows NT system
Port 3: From console of secondary tier #1 (PS/2 mouse)
Port 4: From console of secondary tier #2

Secondary Tier #1 - OmniView 4 port PS/2 model (all use PS/2 mouse)
Console: Goes to Port 4 of top tier
Port 1: RS/6000 Power Series 400 running AIX 4.1
Port 2: Dec AlphaStation 200 4/166MHz running Digital UNIX 10.0b
Port 3: Sun Sparc 10 running 2.5.1
        Requires a 13W3-to-VGA adapter and a Sun Interface Converter.
        The Sun Interface Converter allows a PS/2 keyboard and PS/2
        mouse to be connected to the Sparc 10.
Port 4: HP 9000 Model 712 running HP/UX 10.2

Secondary Tier #2 - OmniView 6 port AT model
Console: Goes to Port 3 of top tier
Port 1: Lugable system
Port 2: Linux system 2.0.30
Port 3: SCO 5.0.4
Port 4: spare
Port 5: spare
Port 6: spare

Notes:
1. Wrong cableing blew out a OmniView switch box.
2. A bad cable fried an OmniView's transformer.
3. OmniView + "Y" splitters mouse and keyboard functions were
erratic until the Top Tier and Secondary Tier #1 were put next
to each other. Don't know why.
4. If cables are detached, the UNIX systems keyboard and mouse
functions may become erratic. Reattach cables and reboot.
5. This setup requires almost 50 cables of various types!
6. I'm successfully using 25 and 50 foot cables to the
Windows computers and the console of the Secondary Tier #2.
Seems contrary to (3). Also used 25 foot cables from the "Y"
splitters to the monitor/keyboard/mice.
7. The Panasonic S17 monitor couldn't handle the HP 712 default
monitor signal of 1280x1024 at 76Hz. Instead, had to use the CTX
monitor to change the HP 712 "bios" to select 1280x1024 at 60Hz.
8. If you use a monitor "Y" splitter, make sure both monitors can
handle the worst-case resolutions and frequencies. Same problem
with mice.
9. The Intellimouse driver for Windows NT doesn't recognize
non-MS Intellimouse mice. The mouse is lifeless.

Best sources for parts:
1. Obtain Belkin OmniViews from Merisel, CDW, or Ingram Micro.
2. Dalco Electronics has 13W3-to-VGA adapters for $17.25. Part
number is 51420. Call them at 800-445-5342 or www.dalco.com.
3. Dalco Electronics has a complete offering of cables.
4. Sun Interface Converters for attaching PS/2 keyboards and mice
to Sun systems are available at SunExpress. $75. 800-873-7869.
http://www.sunexpress.sun.com.
5. I used "Y" splitters from P.I. Engineering, Inc. I bought
through Ingram Micro. The splitters have cute names:
        Y-see two (monitor splitter)
        Y-key key (keyboard splitter)
        Y-mouse (serial mouse splitter)
        Y-mouse (PS/2 mouse splitter)

-- 

