> 
>      There is a big difference between a network interconnect and a
>      disk interconnect.  Since the physical platter bandwidth is only
>      5-10 MBytes/sec best case, and since any given disk array tends to be 
>      topologically close-in, the disk interface is almost certainly
>      going to remain SCSI for a long time to come.  You can use fiber 
>      to connect disk arrays together, sure, but you do not use fiber to 
>      connect disks together.  There is no gain.  Disks just aren't fast
>      enough to warrant the expense.

Agreed, for the short term.  But Box Hill is hot on the trail of 
an all-optical box, both on the host connect side, and on the
disk connect side as well.  The advantage is that with the 
fiber disk connect side, you remove most of the chain length
limitations, and current SCSI addressing issues.  If you've
got a box with 200 disks in it, you don't really want to 
have to divide that up into 30 scsi chains and manage
SCSI id's across all those chains.  Within 18 months, we'll
start seeing fiber connects at the disk level as well on the
very large disk farms, just to avoid the problems and limitations
of SCSI.

You DO use fiber to connect disks together if you have large
numbers of them.  The expense of multiple SCSI controllers
and the headaches associated with them are enough to warrant 
the extra cost of the optics.

