:>fiber disk connect side, you remove most of the chain length
:>limitations, and current SCSI addressing issues.  If you've
:>got a box with 200 disks in it, you don't really want to 
:>have to divide that up into 30 scsi chains and manage
:>SCSI id's across all those chains.  Within 18 months, we'll
:>start seeing fiber connects at the disk level as well on the
:>very large disk farms, just to avoid the problems and limitations
:>of SCSI.
:>
:>You DO use fiber to connect disks together if you have large
:>numbers of them.  The expense of multiple SCSI controllers
:>and the headaches associated with them are enough to warrant 
:>the extra cost of the optics.

    I think you are overestimating the cost of a SCSI bus and the size
    disk arrays need to be to accomplish particular tasks.

    First of all, there are no SCSI address numbering issues in large
    disk arrays... each disk drive is configured from its label and
    can often be put *anywhere* in the array.  You just make sure
    there is no address conflict (trivial) and plop it in.  Even better,
    the slot itself configures the address and you don't have to touch
    any jumpers on the drives.  Just plop them in willy nilly and they
    work.  It's just not a problem.

    Second, There is a chain length limitation but it's not really an issue
    with modern drives.  You can actually fit 15 of the buggers on a single
    SCSI bus and that's all you care about.  Hell, SCSI is so friggin cheap
    that we usually do not put more then 10 on a bus.   It's irrelevant.

    Third, there is a huge cost involved with doing a fiber interconnect 
    verses a copper one, and being able to hang more devices off of one 
    physical network is not a good enough reason for doing it.  The rule
    of thumb for fiber is that you don't do it unless you need to do it,
    and until disk drives start transfering 100 MBytes/sec (1 GBit), it's
    a waste of money to build a fiber interface into a drive.
    Rather then spend $100 for a SCSI controller every 15 slots, you are 
    now spending $40 more per disk drive on fiber interfacing: cabling, 
    transceivers, and chipsets, and probably spending even more on the 
    master controller's DMA and bus interface because all that data
    comes together at a single point.  This is a major scaleability issue.

    Remember, the main limitation with a SCSI bus is not usually the
    bandwidth, it's the transaction rate.  An ultra-wide SCSI bus can
    do 500-1000 (educated guess) complete transactions/sec.  That means
    that the controllers in each drive and the main controller must be able
    to do this too.  With fiber, the controllers in the drives have to be
    able to do 10,000 or 100,000 transactions/sec and the main controller
    also has to do that because you are putting more devices on the 
    fiber.  This is very expensive.  This is extremely expensive compared
    to what it costs with normal SCSI.

					-Matt




