In article <34302810.842CC8F2@jaws.greatwhite.com>, Eddie Esqueda <eesqueda@jaws.greatwhite.com> writes:
>I was wondering if there are any significant differences between OpenVMS
>& AlphaVMS. My company is migrating from a VAX 4705, running OpenVMS, to
>an Alpha( model ??) running AlphaVMS.

Well, to start with, there is no "AlphaVMS", Digital usually writes
it as OpenVMS Alpha, although I think I've also seen them use 
OpenVMS/Alpha too.

>Is there going to be a lot for me to "re-learn?" Is the user interface
>similar or different?

Since the OpenVMS on your alpha will likely be a more recent vintage than 
that on your VAX, you will see some of the newer OpenVMS features, but 
those aren't VAX vs. Alpha changes, and in any case, everything you used
to do should still work the same, it's just that there are a few new things
you can also do.

If you are programming there are a few minor things to be aware of, mostly 
that memory page sizes are no longer 512 bytes (disk pages still are.)
If you're at 7.x or higher there are also 64 bit issues to consider
(or ignore, if they don't matter to your code.)

My advice, don't worry about the user interface, the only real difference 
you'll see is the speed up.  However, if you want more info, have a look at:

  http://www.openvms.digital.com/cgi-bin/readtext.exe/openvms/spd/text/2501.txt?Software+Product+Description

Regards,

