screen.bobsun@worldnet.att.net wrote:

: Optical fibers don't glow, jacketed or not. 1) The laser/LED sources are
: infrared. 2) The whole purpose of a fiber is to couple the light into
: modes which don't "leak" out of the fiber.

	Exactly.  The fiber is made in such a way that the indices of refraction
of the fiber and its coating keep the light waves inside the fiber itself.  If
the fiber "glowed", it would be incredibly inefficient, as you would need 
repeater stations every few meters.


: Fibers today, glass or plastic, are very cheap - only a few cents/foot.
: It is the connectors and the optoelectronics that cost money. You have
: to be carrying a lot of data to justify the added cost.

	To give some hard data (I worked at a fiber optic cable manufacturing
plant last summer) the highest quality level of fiber that we dealt with (which
was pretty darn high quality stuff) cost 19 cents a meter, or about six cents
a foot.  Contrast this with a relatively low quality connector (lots of loss
and noise at the splice) that cost $800.  So, for a hundred-foot fiber with
bad connections at both ends, you end up paying $1606, of which $6 is the 
actual fiber.

