locked. The situation is revertable. It can happen to any account. It
happens as a sideffect of administrator's setting of life/expiration
time of an account, using dxaccounts or doing it manually.

IN DETAILS
----------

I have encountered a bug in Digital UNIX 4.0A. This bug concernes
Enhanced Security option of the OS.

If you set expiration time / life time for passwords greater than
certain value (I tried 2 years), account gets locked. When you revert
the value back to something more usual (like 100 days), it's OK again.

I have caused the bug to appear through [dxaccounts] CDE application.
This application allows the administrator to set anything for
life/expiration time of user's password (days, weeks, months or years).
And it is correctly translated into entries in <prpasswd> files
(/tcb/files/auth.db and /var/tcb/files/auth.db). The entries u_lifetime
and u_expire (I think I remembered it right) have much larger value than
usual. I have had the bad luck of doing it on root account and had some
fun time around it.

In my opinion, the problem is in the authentication subsystem itself. It
incorrectly interprets such large values and consideres the account
locked.

I have expirienced this on Digital UNIX 4.0A only. I still haven't tried
it on 4.0B, but I will.

Any coments from users, administrators and Digital's experts are welcome

Nix.

