:In article <60r8a0$317@usenet.srv.cis.pitt.edu>,
:Gregg E Economou <geest3+@pitt.edu> wrote:
:>In article <60ph17$4ou$1@nntp.Stanford.EDU>,
:>Matthew Petach <mpetach@nyx.Stanford.EDU> wrote:
:>>> :>SCSI is dead for large disk installations.
:>
:>SCSI was never the thing for largeinstallations to begin with!
:>recently , yes, raids with decent capacity have been moving
:> into that market, but for an installation in a serious shop, 
:>you probably wont see SCSI. DSSI, maybe, SMD in older sites, surely,
:>weird IBM  stuff where those things live, but not SCSI. 
:>SCSI was and is the *Small* computer systems interconnect. ;)
:>So, its a non-issue. 
:>
:>isildur

    No.  SCSI *was* *originally* designed for small computer systems.  When
    it was still a 5 MByte/sec bus protocol and small 'desktop' hard drives
    were still of the 80MByte variety.  Today it's a 40 MByte/sec bus protocol
    in mass-volume production with more then a decade of serious development
    work behind it, and the 'small' hard drives now have multiples of capacity 
    over the big monstrosities of 10-15 years ago.

    When you can throw almost a quarter of a terrabyte on one SCSI bus without
    lun's, and multiple terrabytes with lun's, you can no longer say that 
    it's a small systems standard.

    'Recently' in this case is equivalent to 'around 4 years ago'.  Only a 
    fool wastes money on those older technologies.  Even for medium sized
    installations (20 or 30 4 or 9G drives) on minicomputers, SCSI is the
    only cost effective game in town.

						    -Matt




