Eddie Esqueda wrote:
> I was wondering if there are any significant differences between OpenVMS
> & AlphaVMS. My company is migrating from a VAX 4705, running OpenVMS, to
> an Alpha( model ??) running AlphaVMS.
> 
> Is there going to be a lot for me to "re-learn?" Is the user interface
> similar or different?

Let us start by getting the terminology rigth. VMS was renamed to
OpenVMS when it was introduced on Alpha and when it went to
5.5 on VAX. It was purely a rename invented by brainless
sales-people to make it sell better. We talk about
OpenVMS Alpha and OpenVMS VAX, but also use the short forms
VMS Alpha and VMS VAX. Before VMS 6.2 AXP was used instead of Alpha.
This was also changed due to the same stupid sales-people. So
in short Digital sales has done a great job of confusing all
customers.

Back to VAX -> Alpha migration. It depends. Basicly NOTHING HAS
CHANGED AND EVERYTHING IS AS BEFORE. But ofcourse it depends
a little.

VAX and Alpha are two different architectures, which are not binary compatible,
so you will need Alpha versions of all software. The changes in
"user-DCL" and "system-management-DCL" is close to zero. It is about
the same as 5.5 -> 6.1 on VAX. There are some new and changed features,
but just small things. If you program own applications, then there
may be bigger changes depending on the code. Some code is just
compile an dlink and voila ported from VAX to Alpha. Other code
is big problems. If you simultaneous migrate from VAX C to
DEC C, then you will get more problems.

                                            Arne



