  Tom,
        I won't pretend to know what specific "customer features" that
windows 95, for example, incorporates.  But I have a stinking suspicion
that you are right on the money: making software cumbersome and riddled
with loopholes makes the entrenched OS the dominant OS.  A case of free
market failure?  I sure hope not: but the only free market solution I
see to the problem is either a) vast number of users and developers
becoming enlightened simultaneously (yeah right) or b) someone invents a
"Wonder-OS" that can learn what loop-holes application software needs to
run on-the-fly.
    Of course, another issue is that if software were too easy to write,
vast hordes of programmers would be jobless and a drag on the rest of
the floor-sweeping masses.  The only reason why I would not recommend
artificially making programming harder is because some other soveriegn
nation might get ahead of us and destroy us with an ultra efficient
virus: the US has gotta keep up with the Informational Arms Race, after
all.  On the other hand, what is wrong with being beaten by the better
man?
    In the end, the bottom line is Microsoft has a monopoly on the most
important information tool today, (the OS of 85% of the most popular
type of PC) and it is there not because of technical superiority, but
because, as you say, of huge consumer momentum (by way of "features" and
"compatability").  That is very very bad.
    But think of this.  In a "rational" world where all the world's
information is at your fingertips with a minimum of real effort,
information becomes homogenous, cultures are destroyed, and private
individuals loose control of their own identities.  The only problem is
that this heterogy should be maintained by competition, not
obfuscation.  If the federal government wanted to break Microsoft up, it
would be a heinous violation of corporate rights: it would also force
users to abandon the crutch of Windoze, and perhaps save the world from
eventual complete and utter homogeneity.
    Of course, this cry of "viv la difference!" comes from atop an
intel/windows/netscape pedestal rather than a powerpc, as it should.

