>The only problem with this is that, under U.S. law, violations of antitrust
>law are not dependent on market share. If you break the law, it doesn't
>matter if you have 1% market share or 99%.

Yes it does, although this depends.  Firms that lack "market power"
(which generally implies low market _share_) are generally incapable
of violating U.S. antitrust law unless they act in concert with
many other little firms (i.e., conspiracy) the aggregate of which
holds market power.

Antitrust is all about preventing the big firms from abusing
their market power.  A firm that lacks market power usually
has nothing to abuse.  Take the nebulous world of predatory
pricing as an example: this particular offense can only be committed
by a "dominant" firm; see Barry Wright Corp. v. ITT Grinnell
Corp., 724 F. 2d 227 (1st Cir. 1983) for an example that I happen
to have in front of me.

