on Fri, 26 Sep 1997 12:10:17, B.Kelderman@dyme.nl (Bas Kelderman) wrote:
> >  I made this partitions:
> >     Partition 1: 130 MB FAT System "C"
> >     Partition 2:4000 MB FAT        "D"
> >               (by the way: how to format NTFS?)
> >
> You cannot use NTFS on an Alpha.
> I also had problems installing NT after using the built in disk
> partitioner, I now started using the ARCINST program on the NT cd
> again and had no problems since.

Actually this is incorrect - you just can not directly boot from NTFS. 
The bootloader needs to be in a partition that ARC understands (ie FAT).
My home UDB (multia) is currently set up with a 20 meg partition for
bootloaders and disk management tools, a 520 meg NTFS partition for NT
and a 1G Jazz formatted with EXT2 for Linux.  The ability of ARC to
directly grab at bootloaders greatly eases the use and maintanence of
multiple OSes (or versions - for a while I had NT 3.51 and 4 on the same
machine) - I'm glad that, for myself, the days of MBRs are gone.  (I
even keep an emergency floppy with the linux/NT loaders with seperate
ARC boot entries in case something happens to my C FAT partition)

As to how to format it NTFS - tell NT to do it during setup (what it
actually ends up doing is formating it FAT and then converting it to
NTFS - screwy process).  NTFS is definitely the way to go - a little bit
speedier - far more secure - and long filenames (though of course poorly
written Windows programs sometimes mess up because of them).  FX!32
really loves NTFS as well.  The one major drawback is that Linux/Alpha
can't currently see that drive.

-scot

