EmmanuelS@hol.fr wrote:
> 
> I have a vobis computer (with AlphaPC164 motherboard).In Vobis France
> they can t help me because they dont know this computer.
>  I have a problem with Windows NT 4. My computer freeze sometimes.
> Why? is it because I have an IDE hard drive? What is the best SCSI
> host adapter and SCSI hard drives ?
> Is there a free 64 bit OS?
> Can I put on this computer a Miro DC30?
> is there a direct X (alpha)?
>                                                       thanks

I would be tempted to joke a little and tell you that you've answered your own
question by mentionning that you chose NT as your OS. That's why your computer
freezes! ;-)

Seriously, first and foremost: NT is *always* much happier with SCSI drives
than IDE. Both Adaptec and Mylex/BusLogic cards are good choices, from my own
experience, at least. I tend to like Seagate drives quite a bit. They are
fast, reliable and have some models that run at 10,000 rpm. With that kind of
rotational velocity, the latency is greatly reduced...They just fly! Avoid
Toshiba, Fujitsu and Conner drives.

If you select a drive that is UltraSCSI, just make sure you select a SCSI
adapter that supports it too. This should save you a lot of frustration in
unexpected drive disconnections or tagged commands not being acknowledged at
the hardware level. Even though a non-ultra device should be able to negotiate
a lower common speed to talk to an Ultra adapter, or vice-versa, in real life
it does not always happen so nicely. Also, if you only have one hard drive on
your SCSI bus, you won't get much performance gain by using a Wide SCSI drive
as opposed to a Narrow SCSI one.

As far as a free 64-bit OS for the DEC Alpha, Linux is exactly what you're
describing, and runs very nicely. Of course it is UNIX, not NT (NT is *not* 64
bits and won't be for a while). Not free, but very nice too is Digital's own
Digital UNIX, which I run on several DEC machines.

Hope this helps

