Um, you're a bit late.  Box Hill is selling fiber based
disk arrays already, IDT and Invincible are re-selling
Clarion fiber based disk arrays.  The cool thing about
the Invincible arrays is they allow up to 99 host
initiators on the same ring, with up to 22km between
nodes, so you can pretty much make your disk chain
shared between all the hosts in a building, for example.
They support hubbing on the fibre as well, with self-healing
interfaces, so you can hot add and remove host initiators
to a disk farm.  Initial speed is 100MB/sec on the models
being released end of this year.

SCSI is dead for large disk installations.

