In article <60bl3h$avu@rc1.vub.ac.be>, VANCRAEYNEST STIJN <tw47295@vub.ac.be> writes:
>
>I would like some information about the DEC 5900 model.
>(equipped with R4000 proc.)

A cabinet configuration of the DECsystem 5000/240.  Or in
your case Model 260.  It uses a 60 Mhz R4000 chip and
supports up to 448 MB of memory.  Prestoserve NVRAM was
part of the standard configuration.  Without the Prestoserve
the maximum memory is 480 MB.  The base system also include
a TURBOchannel extender and the basic three TURBOchannel
slots.  The extended slot might often be used with the
CITCA, the TURBOchannel CI adapter for connecting HSC
family controllers.

The base system module included a Slow/Narrow/Single-ended
SCSI adapter, Ethernet and four line serial interface.  The
base configuration included an RZ58 and RRD42 CDROM.  I
think the base system included a storage drawer that could
be configurated as one or two SCSI busses with slots for
up to seven full height disks.  There were a fair number
of half-height slots which probably cost a full-height
slot.

The TURBOchannel was the full 25 Mhz version.

>
>How old is this model?

The 40 Mhz CPU version is listed in my November 1992 Systems
and Options catalog.  It isn't listed in my April-June 1991
catalog.  So, its about 5 years old.  The R4000 CPU was 
delayed and may have come out as much as a year or two
later.

>Which Intel processors (and which x86 based servers) deliver
>about the same specs&performance as this machine?
>(PPro? dual? Quad? PII?)

First generation, medium speed Pentium maybe?  The base
system is five years old after all and the R4000 upgrade
was late.  My SOC does conviently have the Specmark number
for the 40 Mhz CPU; 32.8.  This was late enough in 1992
that it was probably the 2nd suite based on the 11/780.
The 60 Mhz CPU would probably scale up from that a bit
high; the clock speed difference and some for the R4000.

