Uh, that's an r4400 processor, unless someone plugged a 50MHz
daughterboard for a 5000/1xx or 5000/2x into a 5900 chassis.

[snip]


>  The extended slot might often be used with the
> CITCA, the TURBOchannel CI adapter for connecting HSC
> family controllers.

The extender gives three mechanical slots, but they're all still one
TurboChannel slot. That's useful for boards that physically occupied
more than one slot, like the two-slot graphics adaptors or the
three(?)-slot CITCA. The TC ASIC on the motherboard can still only
address three slots.


> The 60 Mhz CPU would probably scale up from that a bit
> high; the clock speed difference and some for the R4000.

I recently calibrated delay loops for the in-kernel DELAY() function
in NetBSD.  The 60MHz r4400 CPU executes buzzloops four times faster
than the 40MHz r3000.

Both the 50MHz r4000 CPU and the 60MHz r4400 CPU daughterboards have
1Mbyte L2 cache. Both are superpipelined, and the r4000 has
8kI-+8kD-cache, the r4400 has 16kI+16kD cache.

I'd guess that depending on the workload, the r4400 would rate
anywhere between a 75MHz to (for FP-intensive work) a 133MHz Pentium.
Your mileage may vary.



