A long time ago I was the lead hardware engineer on the RQDX3
controller. I'll try to help with what I remember.
> 
> Questions:
> 
> 1)  Please provide some assurance, if it is warrented, that the ROMs can be
>     updated in this situation.  (I figure that if "newer" ROMs were approprate
>     for an older board -- and I don't remember when they were updated -- then
>     they have to be better for all boards from that one forward (unless even
>     newer ones were required).)  Can anyone tell me the earliest and latest
>     ROM revisions that are usable in the various RQDX3 board types?

Yes the ROMs can be upgraded on the all the modules. There were only
minor bug fixes or additional device support added. Nothing that would
cause the problem that you are
seeing.  Use the latest ROMs that you have. I don't remember the final
version number of the microcode.

> 
>     (I would be more specific here, but I forgot to write down the numbers,
>      and I can't strip apart the computer at the moment.)
> 
> 2)  Please provide some assurance that there is no EEPROM on the RQDX3
>     that can only be set with the damned-if-we'll-give-it-to-you service
>     diagnostic program that's required to format the RD series disks.
>     (That's why I gave up on the RD disks to begin with.)  I would have
>     hoped that if there was such an EEPROM, it would be socketed -- and
>     I would be able to swap it -- but so far, no such luck.

There is no such EEPROM or function that prevents you from formatting.
You do however have to use the diagnostic formatting program that was
shipped with the system. What happens with the RQDX3 is that the
formatter program knows what type of device you are trying to format and
after formatting the tracks the drive geometry is written to a reserved
data block (head 0, track 0, sector 0).  

When the RQDX3 microcode looks for devices it reads this reservered data
block to get the drive geometries.  This allowed us to add new drive
support without performing a field microcode upgrade. All that was
required was to get the latest formatter that knew about the new device.

What has happened in your case is that the reserved data block is no
longer readable and the controller reports a default device type of
RD51.  It may not have been a head crash, just a random write when the
original controller failed.  A reformat would be the first thing that I
tried.

> 
> 3)  As for the remaining fault (invalid media format), there are two
>     possibilities.  One is that the new (used) RQDX3 card is faulty.
>     The other is that the original card smashed the disk on the way down.
>     I tend to believe the former, since even if the content of the disk was
>     smashed the controller should still recognize it as an RD53.  On the other
>     hand, this may be one of the mysteries of the service diagnostic program,
>     or that both sets of ROMs that I have are too old for the newer card.
>     Does anyone have any similar experiences that may shed some light on
>     the situation?
> 
> Thanks for sharing your experiences,
> Jonathan Hardis
> 


Nick Warchol

