In article <3429D491.6E3655EA@tfs.net>, Jim Bryant  <jbryant@tfs.net> wrote:
>What ever happened to bacteriorhodopsin?


I mentioned that one of the best candidates is a protein. 
However there are problems with it, though at least in the lab
at IBM's geneva research center they have built storage devices
based on bacteriorhodopsin. The thing there is that a: it is sensitive
in different ways to different wavelengths, b: its state-change is 
not extremely fast, c: driving the device too fast will break down the 
protein and lose data, d: the application of this substance is more
suited to storage in a massive way than to processing. 

It is interestign to note the very obscure research done in the Soviet
Union in the mid to late eighties in protein-based signal processors 
and analog computers. They reputedly built working analog signal processors
and simple digital components out of proteins. This stemmed from a
realization in the mid seventies that the USSR would take forever to catch
up with western semiconductor technology and a small budget was
allocated to research leapfrogging semiconductors altogether and 
investigate the possibility of processing devices made out of organic
chemicals. 
Unfortunately, in the meantime, market issues have rendered competition
to semiconductors as unable to compete, and performance explosion
in conventional silicon has not let up yet, and of course there hasnt been
much of any research in the ex-USSR since 1990 ;)

Still, silicon wil reign until it is no longer the cheapest thing on the
market. Semiconductor memories were on the market for eight years before
they serious impacted the market share of core memory, because it wasnt til
1973 that the cost of silicon memory came down to the price of core. 
It will be the same with whatever comes along to replace silicon. 

isildur




