Sidney L.. Schofield wrote:
> 
> I have several hundred tapes, and tape images that were written with RXS-11M
> bru, and flex routines, as well as VAX bac.  I no longer have my VAX, nor my
> RSX-11 machines.  Are there any utilities out there that will allow me to read
> these backup sets on a unix machine?  Any help would be appreciated.
> 
> Thanks
> Sidney@coastal.ufl.edu
Have a look at vmsbackup.* in ftp.wku.edu in [.vms.free-vms] if I recall
rightly. It reads vms backup tape.

FLX tape is DOS format. DOS-11 tape has a 14 byte header (7 pdp11
16 bit words). Lessee if I can recall the format

fil
nam
ext
uic
date
prot
e  (last 3 chars of filename, used by FLX only; dos-11 supported
6.3 character names)

Date was something like days since 1/1/70 but memory is quite
vague about this.

After that, the data would be on tape as 512 byte blocks. For text
files, DOS just stored CRLF terminated text, so just dumping that
to unix and possibly munging the crlf to lf could be enough to get
it back. No fancy ecc, crc, or what have you. Files are separated
by a single tape mark, and double tape mark means end of tape.

Binary files could be raw data or "formatted binary" in which records
were preceded with a header giving record size and attributes
followed by data. I no longer recall details about that format,
which was used for .OBJ files. I don't recall for sure what .LDA
files looked like, though in DOS V10 they were often contiguous
and just pure memory images after a header. I believe .CIL files
were also pure binary.

.TSK files from RSX would be pure binary also, but you had to tell
RSX how to store binary files. It understood a few filetyp es
automatically and often the others got screwed up. (Virtual RT11
would have been more widely available in 1978 or so if this hadn't
happened to a .VIR file.)
glenn



