I have just acquired an Alpha 4100 with DEC UX 4.0 which will replace my
HP 9000 model 870.   Throughout my UNIX career I have been pleased with
the way that 'model' scripts were used to initialize and control the
configuration of various printer types.  Because everything is in
scripts, it is all available, understandable, and changeable.  If the
script I needed for a particular printer was not around I could go find
it or modify the one I had to provide the functions I need.  As long as
I had the printers manual, I could get the results I wanted with a
little work.   

My problem is that I cannot find these 'model' scripts anywhere on my
Alpha.  On my HP and my old SCO boxes they are in /usr/spool/lp/model
(for the base type available) and /usr/spool/lp/interface (or the
individually configured scripts for each printer).  Where is this stuff
on the 4100 ?  On my HP if I wanted landscape printing I entered "lp
-olanscape" and the scripts sent the appropriate characters.  On the
Alpha I configure a printer as HPIV and that is my only choice.  The -o
option on the lp command allows me to change the printer parameters on
the fly in a readable manner.  I cannot believe that after all of these
years DEC has now made printing more difficult than it used to be.

Please let me know if there are ways to accomplish what I need to do.

