In article <handleym-2209970133480001@handma.apple.com>,
handleym@apple.com (Maynard Handley) wrote:

[Stuff Deleted]
> Come on, let's get real. The reason machines like this don't exist
> electronically is not because they're hard/impossible to make. It's
> because no-one knows of anything useful to do with them. Apart from a few
> very specialized situations, massively parallel programming (and I stress
> the massively) remains a dead end. Probably one day we'll find something
> useful to do with them, but right now they're a distant research issue.
> If this is the best argument people can come up with for optical
> processing, it's no surprise it's a minor field.

Er, Maynard, actually, massively parallel computers are being used
extensively.  A lot of the digital special effects houses use MPP's to
render their images.  Finite element analysis (FEA) is being used by a lot
of designers on MPP's to predict design behavior.  How about weather
prediction?  There are a lot of uses for a MPP.

What keeps people from using MPP's is not the programming nor the lack of
utility.  It's the cost.  For example, a CM5 MPP used to cost $200 million
(back before Connection Machines went out business).  I'm sure a lot of
animators and engineers would buy a 1024x1024 processor machine today if
they cost $10k.

Imagine 1024x1024 processors in a Nintendo?  You could have real time
rendering on a XGA screen.  You don't think a lot of people would kill to
have something like that?  

Note that all of the examples that I've listed are SIMD (single
instruction, multiple data) applications.  I've listed these because, 1)
there are lots of SIMD applications that are useful, 2) the programming
for SIMD has been solved, and 3) optical computers would excel primarily
at SIMD apps.  Of course you could have MIMD optical computers, but bit
plane processing lends itself natuarlly to SIMD computation.

-Chuck



