In article <3427b55a.5111245@news.indigo.ie>, dywalshNOSPAM@indigo.ie wrote:

> O.k. but what about an optical memory bus? One, or a small number of
> optical fibres, could carry the data at very high rates without the EM
> interference, signal races etc. associatated with coppr buses.

Slowly but surely optics is becoming the main communications medium
between electronic processing units.  Used to be that all communications
between computers were carried by copper wire.  Now at the WAN/LAN level
optical fiber is supplanting electrical wire as the communications
medium.  The next level is within the computer itself.  A number of
companies/universities are investigating the possibility of an optical
backboard.  Finally, at the cpu level, a lot of people are looking at the
possibility of having the CPU clock distributed optically to solve clock
skew problems at 1GHz+ speeds.   Beyond that, it's not known how deeply
optics will diffuse into the computation technology.  

-Chuck



