[applications of neural networks]

> 
> Name five that matter in the real world.
> Look, I am not claiming this stuff will never be important. I AM saying
> that here and now, and for at least the next ten years, 99.9% of people
> who use computers (either as users, techs or programmers) have never
> interacted with and will not interact with an MPP system, neural net or
> otherwise.

Well...

The MADELINE network has been used for decades in enhancing telephone
transmissions. I believe that a variant is used in most modems.

There are networks in use in radar, sonar and other pattern-recognition
areas.

There are networks in use in investment programming.

There are networks (not necessarily neural) in use in speech recognition.

There are networks in use in OCRs and handwriting recognition.

There are networks in use in all sorts of areas.

Cheap, *trainable* optical-MMP machines would make an already booming field
grow at an even greater rate since the only really practical trainable
large-scale networks are all computer simulations, which are inherently
slower than MMP systems. If a low-cost, MMP system coud be devised that
would allow *training*, things would change overnight since the biggest
cost associated with  networks is the training-time.



