In article <3427b55a.5111245@news.indigo.ie>,
	dywalshNOSPAM@indigo.ie (Dylan Walsh) writes:
   On Thu, 18 Sep 1997 21:56:04 -0700, this dude,
	screen.bobsun@worldnet.att.net wrote:
   >It takes a waveguide "pipe" microns wide to support an optical
   >mode. If you want to turn a corner, the mode has to be reflected
   >somehow, introducing loss and mode conversion. Optical logic has
   >not been developed to the point of usefulness, consequently
   >logical operations still would have to be done with electrical
   >signals. The required conversion steps introduce significant delay
   >and require power.
   >
   >not an attractive picture at this point.

   O.k. but what about an optical memory bus? One, or a small number
   of optical fibres, could carry the data at very high rates without
   the EM interference, signal races etc. associatated with coppr
   buses.

I would guess that the gain in capacitance avoidance would be entirely
lost be the translation between electronics and optical.  That
translation makes a lot of sense for cases like fiber cable - one
translation for many kilometers of transmission, so the change in
latency is worth it.  But for a motherboard, your latency is _very_
tight, and doubling it won't help anything.  After all, the main
hang-up with memory and the like currently is not the bandwidth to the
CPU - it's the latency for that first transfer.  Bandwidth can be
fixed, even if you have to put the CPU and the RAM on the same chip -
but the latency is still there, even then.

   On the subject for optical processors, I was talking to a guy who
   is a student studying either optics or optoelectronics, and he said
   a friend of his in a higher year, worked for Intel in a summer job,
   in optoelectronics. He said that Intel were working on optical
   processing for the P8, the generation after the Merced. I can't
   verify that it is true, though.

I would guess that it is true that Intel is working on optical
processors.  But for the P8?  Hmm, well, I would further guess that a
summer intern is unlikely to be privy to such things.  I think it's
more likely that the optoelectronics were being used in networking
devices.

My take on the field of "photonics" (I think that's right) is that
we're unlikely to see fully optical computers of any complexity for
quite some time.  We don't even have the capability today to build an
optical Pentium-level chip if we _wanted_ to.  Beyond that,
semiconductor machines have lots of life left in them.  I don't doubt
that we'll be seeing lots of progress in combining optics with
electronics in the area of communications.  Fiber has turned things
upside-down, now it's the fiber transmission network that is cheap,
and the electronic connections to it that are expensive.

Later,

