Yes, I have read the hype in the computer press, just like anyone else.
However I see no difference between this hype and the hype surrounding
"complexity/chaos". 
Supposedly neural nets (and more generally MPP) can do all these things
human brains can do. So how come no-one in the real world is actually
using them? What you have supplied is a laundry list of places where
neural networks supposedly work well. You have NOT supplied a list of
places where neural networks are ACTUALLY doing anything useful. Last time
I looked into this, neural networks were substancially behind things like
Markov models and dictionary techniques for things like speech or
handwriting recognition. 

Connection machines were not the only people to go bust selling MPP
systems. The same happened to Kendall Square (and some would say the same
happened to Cray [the variant that brought out that Alpha-based MPP
system]). 
Come on, get real. If there were a huge demand for low-CPU-complexity but
massively parallel systems, you could put them together for $20 000 using
ARM cores or suchlike and make a killing.

I am not trying to irritate anyone here. I AM however trying to
distinguish between reality now and what may be in fifteen years.

Maynard

