I will never buy another piece of DEC hardware for one reason: customer
support. Let me fill you in on my experience with DEC support.

I bought a UDB (VX40-F2 and stop laughing) awhile ago and it was working
fine until about two or three months ago. It just quit on me one day, and 
whenever I tried to reboot it, it sat there with the diagnostic light
blinking once in slow sucession (i.e. loading the firmware) but nothing
happend. I checked the RAM and upgraded the firmware(via the failsafe
floppy which worked about one out of every ten times booting it), but to
no avail. So I finally gave DEC a call.

Now, here is where I tell you about why I will never buy DEC equipment
again. I called DEC's main line (toll free thank you) and after navigating
the voice menu hell for about ten or twenty years it seemed, I finally got
to a number to call for workstation tech support. "Great," I thought, I
should dial this number and in no time be up in running or throw my UDB
out, depending on what the problem is. So, I dial the number and get some
more vioce menu hell options and finally get a person. I explained my
problem nice and techinically (I *am* dealing with a trained DEC repair
tech) Well, after waiting a half-hour on the tech to figure out if DEC
*ever* made a UDB (granted they're discontinued) he proptly told me that I
called the  wrong line because the UDB is a PeeCee. Well, I told him I
have a one-of-a-kind model then because it seems to have a 21066 chip in
it. Well with a questionable wit about him, he told me to call a different
number. I did, and again after navigating the maze, saw the light at the
end of the tunnel and got a tech on the line. He told me there is a
service repair shop in my area and to see if they can help me because due
to the UDB being discontinued and it isn't even worth the money sending it
in to DEC's main office. Of course, he had no idea what the UDB was. and
also thought it was a PeeCee. So much for discretion and screening when
hiring employee's...I might has well just been calling McDonald's.

Well, as you might have guessed, I went to my local DEC service place. I
dropped it off and was notified it would be a $45 diagnostic fee! Not
applicable to repair's, not refundable, and just about %20 of what I paid
for the UDB! Well, I needed it to get fixed. I told them the problem and
7 days later (so much for fast turn around time, I guess I should have
been bringing in a 500Mhz Alpha, or bought ten of them to get some
priority) I received a call telling me that my UDB was not working. Well
thanks for the insight into my problem...I knew that already Sherlock!
Upon going there to pick it up, I asked again what was wrong with it and
what was the fix. Well they had no clue what was wrong (gee, isn't that
why I brought it there? TO FIGURE OUT WHAT IS WRONG, you are a *service*
center are you not?) I really didn't need to pay $45 for someone else to
boot it and tell me it's broke. Well this is getting long enough, and I'll
be amazed if anyone read this far.

This whole story just goes to show that DEC has no regard for individuals
or if you don't have a multi-million dollar account, you are just another
pee-on to them. With all the trouble that DEC has had lately in the market
place and their previous lack of support, it looks as though DEC is headed
down "bankrupt lane" and good riddance I say, I'm now a Sun Microsystem's
man....

Cordially,
Josh Lynch
tyme@visi.com


P.S. Please disregard the spelling, it *was* written on a *ex-friends*
Alpha.... ;-)



