On Mon, 22 Sep 1997 19:59:11 GMT, NO_S*P*A*M_skulpa@envirosys.com
(Steven S. Kulpa) wrote:

>DEC is always touting OpenVMS as 7x24 avilability, which I completely
>agree with.  What I'd like to know is how reliable is NT on an Alpha
>platform?  Does anyone know a source for reliable NT reliability
>information?  I'm trying to convince the powers to be that we're
>better off with our OpenVMS system that we would be after porting it
>to NT (not counting the porting effort), but I can't back it up
>without some statistics.

I have a friend who bought (for his customer) an Alpha AXP 150 (one of
the first Alpha boxes ever available) to run SQL Server.  It came with
NT 3.1, and has since been upgraded with newer versions of NT as they
showed up.  He says that system is amazingly reliable, and has never
given him any trouble (either hardware related or software).

My personal experience is with NT Workstation, and I've found it to be
completely stable too.  Haven't had a crash or forced reboot in over a
year.  Another data point is my client's NT Server that runs Oracle
along with Transaction Server and IIS.  I've been on that project for
6 months now, and the server itself has never crashed.  We've had some
problems with a SQL query "running away with" the Oracle server, but
those are easily recoverable.  I suspect we'd have the same Oracle
problems no matter what O/S we used.

I've worked with VMS for 12 years.  I've used NT since version 3.1
first shipped.  I think you'll have to look at things other than
reliability to "defend" VMS.  Personally I'm seeing a movement away
from VMS among my customer base, and think the VMS glory days are
over.



