>     (That's why I gave up on the RD disks to begin with.)  I would have
>     hoped that if there was such an EEPROM, it would be socketed -- and
>     I would be able to swap it -- but so far, no such luck.

  Nope - the card is disturbingly normal.

> 3)  As for the remaining fault (invalid media format), there are two
>     possibilities.  One is that the new (used) RQDX3 card is faulty.
>     The other is that the original card smashed the disk on the way down.
>     I tend to believe the former, since even if the content of the disk was
>     smashed the controller should still recognize it as an RD53.  On the other
>     hand, this may be one of the mysteries of the service diagnostic program,
>     or that both sets of ROMs that I have are too old for the newer card.
>     Does anyone have any similar experiences that may shed some light on
>     the situation?

  The RQDX3's idea of the drive geometry info is written to the very front of
the disk at format time, so it's sure to be clobbered if anything goes wrong.

