It would make a lot of sense if:
1) your system could utilize a  disc bus speed < ~100Mb/s. Fiber
probably wouldn't make much sense at lower speeds.
2) You had a disc drive with comparable performance. It makes sense to
increase the data rate only to the point where access time begins to
dominate the total timing. Then there's the minor problem of getting
data in and out of the disc drive.
3) You would use an appropriate fiber material. It has to be transparent
in the IR regime, the wavelengths at which high performance emitters and
detectors operate. Polycarbonate and PMMA look pretty good, at least for
short distances.
4) You solve the "trivial" fiber connection problem. Typical fiber
systems today require alignment of the fiber with the detector or
emitter to within a few microns. This requires precision (read
expensive) connectors. Recent advances have reduced cost, however. 
5) you do all of this at a low cost. 
There are a lot of challenges ahead, but fiber will be used eventually,
when these solutions come together. As i recall the acronym used to
describe the technology is POF - plastic optical fiber.

