> In article <609bvd$27m4$1@newssvr03-int.news.prodigy.com>,
> bill davidsen <davidsen@tmr.com> wrote:
> >
> >Quality of compiler? Who knows, in disassembling a MS application I
> >once saw some code which is emitted by gcc, but which I've never
> >seen from the MS compilers. Would they compile their apps with a
> >free compiler?
> 
> That isnt even legal according to the GPL that gcc is distributed under!
> I wonder if the FSF would be willing to take up a case if it can be proven
> that Microshaft is using gcc to generate commercial code wihtout licensing
> the compiler for such a purpose (and im certain that such a license has not
> been made)!!

While I'm loath to defend MicroSoft, I suggest that you read the
GPL. There is no longer any wording in the GPL that even implies that
code generated by a GPLed product may not be used commercially. The
only prohibitions involve the tools themselves.

The GPL contains the following:
"The act of running the Program is not restricted, and the output from
the Program is covered only if its contents constitute a work based on
the Program (independent of having been made by running the Program)."

The belief that the GPL contains such provisions has cause many people
to reject the use of GNU tools out-of-hand and, in turn, distributed
tools and code that were needlessly difficult to port, install, and
maintain.

Please DON'T spread such rumors. If in doubt, the GPL is readily
available and (for a legal document) pretty easy to understand.
