John Rudd <jrudd@cygnus.com> wrote in article
<606kor$3g6$1@cronkite.cygnus.com>...
: In <6068jt$1aq0$1@newssvr03-int.news.prodigy.com> bill davidsen wrote:
: > There's no problem doing this in theory, or even in practice if you
: > don't insist on small right now. You do it with mirrors mounted or
: > polished on the surface of a pizzoelectric lump. When you apply
: > voltage the dimensions of the lump change, and the mirror shines the
: > light somewhere else.
: > 
: 
: 
: Except that this is not really an optical computer, it's basically 
: conventional semiconductor gates joined by optical transmission lines.  This

: is because you're requiring electrical stimulation of the piezoelectric
lump.
: 
: So, basically, the gate not only needs to move the mirror for deflecting the

: light, it needs a photovoltaic cell to sense the light and generate current 
: to the piezeolectric lump.. which is a delay.  You may not see a great speed

: leap that way, because you've got light->current->deformation as 3 stages of

: delay in the gate.
: 
: You'd be better off going with the same optical switches used in fiber lines

: -- take a photovoltaic cell and put it at the end of a line.  Connect it's 
: output to an LCD, and have the "drain" be a new light source on one side of 
: the LCD, and the source/output on the otherside.  When the LCD is
stimulated, 
: it blocks light.  Otherwise its polarization lets light though.  You get 
: something like this:
: 
:                             /=====  output
:                             |
:     input light ============* LCD
:                             |
:                             laser
: 
: So, if you've got input light, it hits the *, which is a photovoltaic cell 
: connected to an LCD shutter, causing the LCD shutter to block the light from

: the laser.  And then you just use it exactly like you do a transistor 
: (though, I think I got the 'source/drain' terminology wrong on the
transistor 
: parts.. but I'm just a CS major :-)  ), and is a functional inverter.
: 
: NOR (Not OR):
: 
:                 /========= output
:                 |
:        ---------*
:                 |
:        ---------*
:                 |
:                 laser
: 
: NAND (Not AND):
:                         beam join
:                 /========/======== output
:                 |        |
:         --------* LCD    |
:         --------+--------* LCD
:                 |        |
:                 laser    laser (or 1 beam split laser)
: 
: 
: The problem is that you've got to do things like precision build polarized 
: light sources, beam splits, beam joiners, and propperly polarized LCD 
: junctions, oh, and the mirrors for those 90 degree turns (which mess up your

: polarization if you don't work it correctly).  And you're still doing 
: light->electric->LCD-transition for 3 stages of delay.  But at least you're 
: not dealing with moving parts like mirrors.
: 
: (Btw: this type of gate is pattented by bell labs, if I recall correctly -- 
: it's just not fast enough to be used as a replacement for semiconductor 
: gates, so it's  only used for fiber switches that are controlled by 
: semiconductor computers).
