(Walter Roberson) wrote:

Oh for god's sake. There's an idiot in every newsgroup, isn't there?'

I am well aware that, for example, "plastic" predated the second world
war. However most of the plastics that we actually USE today are
post-second world war and the result of extensive industry effort. Tell
me, when was the last time you used anything made of Bakelite or
celluloid? I suspect pretty much the only significant household plastic
pre-WW II is nylon.

Phone system---last time I checked the current phone system runs on fiber
optics (post 1970) and microchips. This is not even to mention the
cell-phone system.

CD players---based on solid state lasers.

VCRs---based on magnetic alloys developed in the 60s, along with post-60s
plastic for the actual tape, not to mention processing technology for
laying down a metal film capable of handling such a high frequency signal.

TVs---solid-state technology defines both the tuning apparatus and user
interface, and cameras of course are all CCDs now.

US government was involved in pretty much ALL of this.

For someone who is so big on checking facts, might I suggest that you
spend more time on details and less on "ultimate origins". The fact that
iron and glass were used 2000yrs ago does not change the fact that the
float glass process, or steels capable of surviving the environment of a
jet engine were the product of extensive industry research (often with
government money), not the result of some lucky accident by some
individual.
Try looking at history of technology post WW-II --- it's pretty damn amazing.

Let's review.
This discussion started because someone was complaining that DOE was
spending money for research into the next generation of lithography. The
issue is "is the US spending this money a good idea"? My answer is yes,
based on the history of how these things have turned out. 
The discussion has nothing to do with ultimate origins of things, with who
invented what, with social policy or anything else. It has to do with the
issue of "is there decent return on investment for money invested in
science an tecnology in this way". So what if many/most of these things
were initially stumbled upon by some amateur? My point is that they were
improved to their current performance through expensive research.

Maynard



