> :that are all part of the knowledge that's been generated by government and
> :industry in partnership since the second world war. 
> 
> Urrr... No.
> 
> Panati's Extraordinary Origins of Everyday Things, reissued 1989,
> Harper & Row, as ISBN 0-06-096419-7 (paperback)
> 
> page 126:
> 
>     Plastic was actually developed as an inexpensive substitute for ivory.
>   The plastics industry in America was borin in 1868, when a serious
>   shortage of ivory prompted a New England manufacturer of ivory billiard
>   balls to offer a ten-thousand-dollar prize for a suitable substitute.
>     A young printed from Albany, New York, John Wesley Hyatt, met that
>   challenge and won the prize with a product he chistened Celluloid and
>   registered as a trademark in 1872.
>     Hyatt did not actually develop Celluloid himself but acquired the
>   British patent for it in 1868 from a Birmingham professor of natural
>   science Alexander Parkes. Around 1850, Parkes was experimenting with a
>   laboratory chemical, nitrocellulose. [...]
> 
> 
> So:
> A) plastics were not the result of government/industry partnership;
> B) their development was purely commercial; and
> C) they were invented ninety years before WWII.
> 
> 
> The phone system, of course, was invented by Gray or Bell over 100
> years ago (1876) and grew quite quickly as a commercial concern (50,000
> phone lines by 1880). Bell was looking for a telegraph improvement,
> Gray thought of it as a toy.
> 
> CD players were developed first by Sony, whose chairman ordered the
> development of an portable entertainment media that could hold a Beethoven
> symphany.
> 
> VCRs I have less material on at the moment. My recollection is that
> they were developed as for entertainment purposes, a follow-on to
> the earlier VTRs (video tape recorders) which used a different
> recording scheme and wider tapes. I do not presently have information
> on the invention of VTRs. Note that VCRs are aimed at the consumer
> market; professional recording is usually done in other formats
> that have better resolution, stability and colour retention.
> 
> TV is a bit harder to pin down. Nipkow in 1884 perhaps. Jenkins and Baird
> in the 1920s, mechanical systems. Swinton invented the complete electronic
> television system theory in 1908-1911. Depends what you want to call
> "television" who you credit. But it was 70 years ago (1927)
> that Farnsworth developed his practical electronic system under
> private financial backing. No government/industry collaboration,
> and many years before WWII. [They All Laughed, Ira Flatow, HarperPerennial,
> 1992, ISBN 0-06-092415-2; Chapter 9 "Who Really Invented Television?"]
