In article <gjapvq548u5.fsf@nortel.ca>, Jerry Quinn <jquinn@nortel.ca> writes:
|> screen.bobsun@worldnet.att.net writes:
|> 
|> I would think that optical connections would be idea for card and
|> motherboard busses.  If all the cards were connected by optics to the
|> controller, it would seem that the peripheral speed could be greatly
|> improved because you don't have to worry about electrical crosstalk
|> between the bus wires.  Anybody that actually knows something about
|> this stuff feel free to correct obvious and subtle flaws with this.  

The main problem with this is a processing issue.  In order to generate
optical signals you normally need a solid state laser and these generally
use mixtures of Ga/Al/As last time I checked.  In contrast, mainstream ICs
use silicon (which is not well-suited for lasers) and all attempts to use
non-standard technologies such as GaAs for mainstream computers have been
less than successful, to put it mildly.

Interestingly enough though, this week's EE Times refers to a paper which
will be presented at this year's IEDM which involves sticking a Ga/Al/As
optical driver on top of a regular silicon IC to implement optical IOs.
However it's one thing to demonstrate this in a research paper and quite
another to solve all of the (significant) manufacturing and cost issues that
would be involved with a real product.  Maybe something like this will happen
in the future, but it seems unlikely to be very soon IMHO.

Hugh.

(As always, these are personal opinions and nothing to do with Sun, ...)

