In article <606rnm$9pr$1@engnews2.Eng.Sun.COM>, hugh@Eng.Sun.COM wrote:

> The main problem with this is a processing issue.  In order to generate
> optical signals you normally need a solid state laser and these generally
> use mixtures of Ga/Al/As last time I checked.  In contrast, mainstream ICs
> use silicon (which is not well-suited for lasers) and all attempts to use
> non-standard technologies such as GaAs for mainstream computers have been
> less than successful, to put it mildly.

Actually, there are at least two ways to trick silicon into emitting
light.  One is to use porous silicon.  Etch silicon such that there are
lots of open space in it and it'll emit light (although no one is sure
why).  The second is to dope silicon.  Much like we dope silicon today to
affect its dielectric properties, we can dope silicon to affect its
optical properties.  The main dopant that's being investigated is erbium. 
Si::Er LEDs have been demonstrated.  The main problem is that the light
intensity is low, primarily due to the low solubility of Er in Si.  

Today, the most practical methods to integrate microelectronics with
micro-optics is to use a mixture of materials, i.e. Si microcircuits, with
Al/Ga/As/In/P (some mixture of these 5 elements) to make lasers/ LEDs/
photodetectors/ modulators/ etc.  The way to do this is to either just use
two different IC's and wire them together on a board, or actually combine
two IC's together by bonding them.

-Chuck



