A while Back, it seems that Digital was going to win the "video on
demand" war with its Alpha chip and huge storage and memory capacity.

Now, airlines have adopted the technolgy and starting to implement it in
their aircrafts business (and first when available) classes. This is
well before any concrete service on the ground.

Here is some info I gathered from another newsgroup:

[ A list of VoD vendors and their "launch customers" (AFAIK):
[    Sony TransCom:  South African Airways
[    Matsushita:            Singapore  (One aircraft, SIN-NRT)
[    B/E Aerospace:    Japan Airlines, Asiana
[    Hughes:                ??
[    In Flight Tech:        Swissair??

So, how come DIGITAL is not present/visible in there ?????????

That would have been the *PERFECT* place for Digital to show its logo
and 
make itself known. Sure, sales to a few hundred aircraft are not all
that interesting, but think of the thousand BUSINESS folks who would see
Digital's logo on their screens during their long flights EVERY DAY ? Is
there no better way to reach a target audience ?

The systems may en up using ALPHA chips and Digital storage arrays and
DLT tapes (oh, I forgot, DEC sold the DLT stuff), but they will bear
somebody else's name and DEC won't get any marketing bang for its bucks.

Another missed opportunity for Digital.



