>The basic problem with light is that there's no way to make the stupid
>photons stop moving around so much. In a semiconductor, you can push
>electrons into metastable states which you can then go back and
>detect. That's where you get the 0s and 1s. But there's no way to do
>that with "just light".

You're talking about some form of a memory. Well, first of all, 
you have to open your eyes and think for a moment that there is a lot
more than just 'signal'||'no signal' to represent 0 and 1. In a light-based
ssytem polarisation will probably be your best bet. And the light wont 
certainly stick around liek you can make electrons do in a capacitor, for
example, but it _can_ leave a changed state in a polarising unit, which can
be read back later. 
Theres dozens of ways to do the same thing, just that most people hear of
one of them and think thats hwo the universe has to be. geez!
think of the _theme_ of the problem, not the _particular_ implementation
of one technology. The theme will be applicable in a lot of places, whereas
an implementation is usually almost worhtless outside of its own niche. 

