This was a hot topic in the late 80's and early 90's.  A great book for
computer scientists on this subject is "Optical Computing" by Dror
Feitelson.  It's a little dated, but still a good overview/introduction.

There are a lot of purported advantages to using photons as a computing
medium rather than electrons.  The main advantage was that you could
process planes of information (i.e. an image) rather than single bits in
optical computers. Imagine processing 1024x1024 bits of information
simultaneously.  The speedup would be tremendous.  Now, you can do the
same thing with an electronic computer, but it's really hard and
expensive.  The largest parallel electronic machines have on the order of
1024 processors.  There are some a little bigger, like 4k, but certainly
not 1024k.  

There are, however, a lot of challenges with optical processors.  One is
that photons do not interact easily and electrons interact very easily. 
If you are trying to get one stream of information to control/manipulate
another stream (the "essence" of computing), it's really hard to get them
to interact if they're made of photons.  However, getting an electronic
signal to "and" a different electronic signal is easy.  

On the other hand, electrons interact so well, that when transporting
information from one point to another, the electrons require their own
pipes (conductors) and shielding (insulators).  This means that you can
only carry one stream of electronic information per pipe.  Photons, on the
other hand, can transport information in parallel down a pipe.  You can
have a stream of 1024x1024 bits (a bit plane) travelling through a
waveguide.  

As a result, the consensus seems to be, "let's use electrons for
processing, and photons for communicating."  A lot of people believe that
the "high technology" for computation in the next decade is lots of
electronic processors working in parallel with smart optical "free space"
interconnections.  Some even propose having information interact as
electrons at the transistor, and then transporting the information as
photons for the interconnect.  This seems to me to be a good idea taken
too far.  There is a speed/cost/energy/integration penalty associated with
electron/photon conversion.

Optical Computing is a fascinating idea, and lots of really good research
and neato ideas have come out of it.  It's really too bad it's not going
to pan out in the next decade (in my opinion).  I'd love a 1024x1024
parallel computing machine on my desk right now.

-Chuck

