Subject: Benchmarking EDO vs SDRAM, and more
Date: 1997/09/21
Message-ID: <342570C8.E51990A2@tonys.ecology.umn.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 274339747
Organization: University of Minnesota
Newsgroups: comp.sys.intel,comp.sys.dec,comp.benchmarks


Hi everybody

I have a lattice Quantum Chromodynamics code that runs
then reports an average number of Mflops calculated after
a very FPU and memory intensive run. I have run it on 
a 300MHz pentium II with EDO Ram, but would like to see
how fast it runs on a SDRAM machine. If you would like to
test out your new PII, I could send you my source code,
which you can compile and run. I have inline assembly 
routines for the Intel version.

Here are some of the current numbers:

Pentium II 300MHz 128M EDO RAM:      130 Mflops
Alpha 500 MHz 128M SDRAM      :      245 Mflops

It would be very interesting to see what the SDRAM
based PII workstations can do with this code, which 
is extremely memory intensive. (More than 64M of
physical RAM is required). 

Also, there is a NT version that uses multiple CPUs 
effectively. If you have a dual (or more) CPU system, I 
would be interested in those results too. The entire 
source code is about 10,000 lines.

Thanks to Rob Harley, there are routines optimized
for RISC CPUs, so I would also be interested in numbers
for faster Alpha based systems. The last time I checked,
the fastest HP PA-something systems performed the best
on this code, so again I would be interested in seeing 
how it does on the latest and greatest.

I'll post all the results I get back.

cheers

Henley



