In article <5vhpda$slj$1@nuacht.iol.ie>,
	Francis J Murphy <guest@iol.ie> writes:
> Q1 I was given 2 Vaxstations free by PC expert.

The /30 and /40 aren't the fastest systems around, although they're
quite interesting little beasts.

> There is no software on them.
> Where can I obtain software.

The only OS' that'll currently work on them are the Digital-supplied
VMS (latest version v7.1) and the (discontinued, AFAIK) Ultrix (last
version 4.5, classic BSD flavour) which won't work on a /76.  These'll
obviously cost yer, but for a business environment you can do a hell
of a lot worse.  Applications for these OS' are pretty voluminous.

There're two other systems which should run on these which are in the
pipeline; the NetBSD crowd are in the process of getting their Unix
up and running on the 3100 series, some success stories have already
come to light, but be prepared to hack!  There're also a team of VAX
devotees who've started a project to get Linux working on this
platform, although this is very much in its infancy.  Curiously enough,
another project was started a while ago to get a free version of VMS
up and running (on unspecified platforms) although the mailing list
went quiet quite some time ago.

> Q2 How will they work in with my Win95 and NT4 Network.

Based on my assumption that you'll have TCP/IP operating, then you'll
have no problem.  There're a variety of IP packages available for VMS,
and, of course, all Unices come with this protocol built in (except
for some irrelevant brain-damaged ones, before someone corrects me)

> My PC guy isn't into Vaxstations.

His loss.  OTOH, many computer guys aren't into PCs.

Chris.



