In article <01bcc52d$40837b10$a201ef8c@ubu>,
Mark Szamrej <szamrej@no-spam.centerline_dot_com> wrote:

| I'm not an "expert" in the field but I did alot of work in hardware a few
| years ago. As I understand it, light is "cool" and works great. The main
| problems they were having (last time I checked) was with fabricating the
| basic/fundmental building blocks using optical components.
| 
| Example:
| 
| Logical gates... you know, the OR, AND, NOT, XOR, NAND, etc, etc.
| 
| These types of logical "building blocks" are used to create larger "building
| blocks" like flip-flops, latches, counters and etc.
| 
| The problem is building these blocks with "optical" components which we can
| easily do with semi-conductors. A good example would be an "AND" gate. The
| logical equivalent of two switches in "series" with each other. Both switches
| (switch 'a' AND switch 'b') have to be "ON" for the circuit to conduct. 
| 
| Since transistors are essentially "switches" this is easy. How do you do this
| with light?
| 
| Or an "inverter" (NOT) function. NO light "in" yields a positive light "out".
| How do you do this??
| The other half of this inverter is easy. Light "in" yields nothing "out". I
| can do that with a sheet of paper !!

There's no problem doing this in theory, or even in practice if you
don't insist on small right now. You do it with mirrors mounted or
polished on the surface of a pizzoelectric lump. When you apply
voltage the dimensions of the lump change, and the mirror shines the
light somewhere else.

For AND you have mirror A (input) reflect light to mirror B (input)
to location C (output). For OR either reflect to C, for NOT just
move the output so the light hits there when voltge is not applied
to the input. XOR can be built from the above in the usual way.

The transducers can run at frequencies high enough to do computing,
some are used for ultrasound (500MHz) applications, and that was a
target frequency, not a limit.

To make a practical computer would take doing the engineering to
downsize this, mount little LEDs and CCDs, etc. However, it *is* an
engineering problem, not requiring new physics. I would expect to be
able to build a small system as proof of concept for thousands
rather than millions, using full size components available today.
--
