In article <5vsi23$vl2@camel1.mindspring.com>,
Joe Castle <jmcastle@mindspring.com> wrote:

|  I openly admit that I'm no engineer, but what about a "light" based computer? 
|  Somehow using fiberoptic buses or something?  Seems using light would solve 
| alot of heat/speed problems.

The purported advantage of using light is that you can pass multiple
streams of photons through the same space, while you can only have a
single conducting trace at any given point. I'm not sure that anyone
has made use of this, or that it's actually an issue.

Note that the smallest feature size was known in the 60's, and
described in an article which I wish I had saved. The problem is
that a trace below a certain size is no longer a conductor in the
usual sense, but an exercise in probability. I believe that means
that the timing becomes indeterminant rather than that the charges
don't ever get propigated, but I'm not that kind of physics dude, I
could be wrong.

|  Any hi-tech comments on this?

Not from me. There is a limit, we're no where near it, and after we
reach the limit we will have to go to parallel processing rather
than faster CPUs. I don't expect to see that be a problem in my next
few incarnations ;-)
