I'm not an "expert" in the field but I did alot of work in hardware a few
years ago. As I understand it, light is "cool" and works great. The main
problems they were having (last time I checked) was with fabricating the
basic/fundmental building blocks using optical components.

Example:

Logical gates... you know, the OR, AND, NOT, XOR, NAND, etc, etc.

These types of logical "building blocks" are used to create larger "building
blocks" like flip-flops, latches, counters and etc.

The problem is building these blocks with "optical" components which we can
easily do with semi-conductors. A good example would be an "AND" gate. The
logical equivalent of two switches in "series" with each other. Both switches
(switch 'a' AND switch 'b') have to be "ON" for the circuit to conduct. 

Since transistors are essentially "switches" this is easy. How do you do this
with light?

Or an "inverter" (NOT) function. NO light "in" yields a positive light "out".
How do you do this??
The other half of this inverter is easy. Light "in" yields nothing "out". I
can do that with a sheet of paper !!

Lately it seems that optical processors have been put on the shelf until we
can capture some more quality "alien" technology. <g>

- Mark -




