In article <5vprul$q5c$2@news.ccit.arizona.edu>, Gordon C Zaft <zaft@u.arizona.edu> writes:

:	I'm pretty sure ALLL the VAXstation models had both an AUI and a
:thin ethernet connection, so they are all 'networkable'.

  Well, I'll wager I've got more grey hair than Gordon does.  :-)

  A number of VAXstation systems -- but certainly not all of the various
  VAXstation systems ever sold -- will have both AUI and BNC (ThinWire)
  network network connections available.  Though there are VAXstation
  systems around with no network controller(s) installed.

  Some older VAXstation systems are configured with no network controller.

  And various older VAXstation systems are more likely to be configured
  with network controllers that support only AUI connections, with no
  ThinWire BNC.

  Typical systems seen without network controllers can include those
  based on the KA630, KA650, and KA655 CPUs.  Some older members of
  the KA4% series are also (more rarely) configured without network
  controllers.  These CPUs are found on the VAXstation II, III, 3200,
  3500, III+, and VAXstation 3100 series.  Many of these systems will
  have network controllers, but not all of them...  And I would expect
  to find various VAXstation 2000 and various Q-bus-based VAXstation
  systems configured without network controller(s).

