  Hmm..  I remember a japanese company working on optical storage which
used crossed beams of light to polarize bits in the medium such that a
third beam of light would be blocked or not blocked when trying to "read"
that position in the medium.  I was still in school when I heard about 
it, so it had to have been prior to 1994.

  I'm a software guy myself, but it seems like this might be a good angle
to attack the problem from.  If a fast means of polarizing/depolarizing
material with light could be found such that it blocks or passes light
from a different angle, we could build all of those logic parts.
  Imagine:  two adjacent photoreactive-material units, polarized by the
shining of light through them from direction A, such that in the polar-
ized state they block light from direction B.  The two units are aligned
such that the same beam of light can shine through them from direction B
simultaneously.  Now if input A0 is "on" (ie, a beam of light is shining
through unit 0 from direction A) or input A1 is "on" (ie, a beam of 
light is shining through unit 1 from direction A), then the output will
be "off" (ie, a beam of light shining through both units from direction
B will be blocked by one or the other).
  Alternatively, the output could shine through the units in parallel,
resulting in an unblocked light stream in the B direction iff both A0
and A1 are on.

  Speed of polarization would be the biggest problem..  From what I
recall about the optical storage project, reading was fast but writing
was slow because it required time for the medium to polarize.  Also,
the more quickly you polarized/depolarized a given unit, the more heat
would have to be generated (thermodynamic law).  I'm guessing optical
computers would take decades of research before they'd be more practical 
than electronic computers.

  -- ttk@cygnus.com

