In article <5vsi23$vl2@camel1.mindspring.com>,
Joe Castle <jmcastle@mindspring.com> wrote:

>  I openly admit that I'm no engineer, but what about a "light" based
>  computer?  Somehow using fiberoptic buses or something?  Seems
>  using light would solve > alot of heat/speed problems.  Any hi-tech
>  comments on this?

The basic problem with light is that there's no way to make the stupid
photons stop moving around so much. In a semiconductor, you can push
electrons into metastable states which you can then go back and
detect. That's where you get the 0s and 1s. But there's no way to do
that with "just light".

You might be able to use light for communications busses, but the
function of semiconductors is not likely to be replicated with a "pure
light" solution for a long time.

Rick R.



