In <5vsi23$vl2@camel1.mindspring.com> Joe Castle wrote:
[munch]

->  I openly admit that I'm no engineer, but what about a "light" based 
computer? 
->  Somehow using fiberoptic buses or something?  Seems using light would 
solve 
-> alot of heat/speed problems.
-> 
->  Any hi-tech comments on this?

As it happens, there was quite a bit of work going on in this area in the 
'80s, but I haven't heard a whole lot about it lately.  The really 
fascinating device I remember reading about was something called a 
"transphaser" which was a crystal which had the property that the presence or 
absence of light on one facet would determine whether light could pass 
through it along a different axis.  It was described as a purely optical 
switch, analogous to a transistor used in a digital circuit.

I also remember reading about someone demonstrating a half-adder built up 
from pure optical switches.  Of course, it's a long way from a half-adder to 
a CPU, but it was very promising.

Ultimately, though, it comes down to a race.  At one time we thought 
Magnetic-bubble memories were going to be the wave of the future, but 
conventional semiconductor RAM and magnetic Disks improved so much, so fast 
that the advantages of Bubble memory were completely obviated for most 
applications.

For now, electronics are still improving at a very impressive rate, and we 
won't really need to go to optics until that progress starts to stall.  We'll 
need to see an advantage about as great as the shift from vacuum tubes to 
solid-state was, before the industry "goes optical."



