In article <5vsi23$vl2@camel1.mindspring.com>,
>
> I openly admit that I'm no engineer, but what about a "light" based computer? 
> Somehow using fiberoptic buses or something?  Seems using light would solve 
>alot of heat/speed problems.
>
> Any hi-tech comments on this?

Yeah. No satisfactory switching material is available. 
There are several candidates, one, a protein, several long
molecule polarisation style materials, similar in operations
to a liquid crystal, but photosensitive in some planes. 
However, these materials are not very suitable for making into 
a product. Also, while in laboratory environments simple computing
devices have been made which operate using light, they have been
in reality hybrid optical-electronic machines, for one, and for another
have not been very directed at geeneral purpose computing but 
highly parallel image processing, and not with very much success.
Yet another con is the _size_ of available switching devices. 
They are orders of magnitude larger than the transistors on an average IC
even of several years ago, and their latency, especially the hybrids, 
 is dismal. 
Heat dissipation? you still have that problem in a fiberoptic cable, though
negnigible. That is, you will never see so much heat that it would even be
really noticeable, whereas an average copper conductor will get warm noticeably
(to an instrument if not to a human, still numerically significant)
rather quickly. 
You also realize that an optical fiber is vastly larger than a line on 
an IC or even a trace on a very dense PCB. 

Also, the 'speed of light' in a device is _not_ the speed of light in a
vacuum( the familiar 300000Km/sec we all memorized in grade school)
In a glass fiber it is significantly lower, though still about twice the
speed of a wavefront in an electrical conductor. 
This only means that you can have (at most) a machine 8 times as big 
(assuming you biuld it to take advantage of three dimensions) and theoretically
have signals get through at the same speed. 

