>I have been given a Micro Vax 2 which is in a largish enclosure,
>it has 3 RD53 drives mounted below a TK 50 tape drive.
>behind the front panel there is a slot for what looks like
>a RX50 mounted on its side. I tried putting one in and the 
>system thought it was DUA3: Is this correct or am I flogging a dead
>horse. BTW its running VMS 5.5  (phaedrus@ultra.net.au)

That's a BA123 microvax-II and the nominal disk controller for hard
and floppies is an RQDX3.  Your problem is if you open it the floppy
is not connected.  RQDX3 can address 4 drives total, any mix of floppy
or hard drives so in your case the three rd53 meand the RX50(two drive
addresses) can't be done.  If you unplug DUA3 you can hook up the
floppy.

One solution would be to find two RD54 (159mb) to replace the
RD53(71mb) drives and move the system to the rd54s. then you can have
the floppy back.

A second solution is to get a second RQDX3 and an M9058 distrbution
board and use that to hook up the floppy as DUB0/1.

