	They can afford NOT to do a lot of things.
	Question:

		When or for whom does the forth coming 21164PC based
		machines factor into purchase considerations?

	Certainly not for me.  I need a PC for home use.  I am not
	a scientist nor engineer interested in a Personal Workstation.
	With the Polaris chip-set, the forthcoming 21164PC will all but
	relegate the traditional UNIX low-end obsolete.  Why?  Because
	the competition (Sun) may be able to compete somewhat on performance
	but won't be able to compete on price.  With ESDRAM a $3000
	21164PC will have 1/2 the latency of Rambus DRAM, twice the
	the performance and equivalent peak bandwidth.  See:

http://www.techweb.com/se/directlink.cgi?EET19970908S0134

	and:

http://www.techweb.com/se/directlink.cgi?EBN19970818S0055

	"The VLSI Polaris with ESDRAM support will dramatically increase Alpha
	PC performance under heavy Windows NT work loads," said Kevin Mankin,
	vice president of VLSI's Enterprise Products division."

	The 21164PC will make a very nice scientific workstation as the
	floating point is about 2.5 times higher than P II and will have
	much better bandwidth.  It isn't for everybody.

	Now are there clouds to that silver lining?  Sure.  Help us out.

	But never mind the 21164PC, when the 21264 gets rolling, can't touch
	it.  It will dominate the high-end for quite a while.  And yes,
	every month it is delayed is 1 month less domination.  Merced looms.
	But Merced won't compete against the 21264 shrink.  Certainly not
	in price nor performance.  Intel will require little in
	the way of advertising.  They own the market, look at the numbers.
	But for serious scientific/engineering computing Alpha will be
	the obvious solution.  Intel is for hobbiests and the general 
	public.
	
	When something is obvious, how much do you need to advertise it?
	Certainly not broadly, just in the niche it is destined for.

				Rob





