  Much of this Intel money goes to subsidizing print ads by PC makers if
  they include the "Intel Inside" slogan along with the "flushing toilet"
  symbol.

> 
> Is DEC going to counter this to save ALPHA ?

  I hope they don't even try.  They would best spend their money on
  getting SA and Alpha into a true 0.25 um process as soon as possible.

  Does Mercedes Benz try to match GM or Ford in huge TV ad campaigns
  to reach the mass market?  What's the point - most people cannot
  afford a Benz anyways.  Similarly most PC buyers won't be buying NT
  systems any time soon.  For those that do most don't need the extra
  performance of an Alpha system.

> 
> Can DEC afford NOT to make a campaign against Intel ?
> Can DEC afford to make such advertising to push its ALPHA ?

  DEC should simply try to make Alpha NT systems price competitive
  with low cost derivatives like the 21164PC, provide chipsets and
  PC class motherboard reference designs, continue to improve the
  compatibility and performance of FX!32, and encourage (maybe even
  subsidize) the creation of as many native Alpha NT versions of
  popular apps as possible.

  If DEC can continue to retain a substantive performance edge over x86
  while staying close in price then hopefully it can cream the high end
  of the NT PC market, especially for technical workstation.  It isn't
  hard imagining many Alpha NT systems being bought for the prestige of
  having the fastest system or simply by standing out from the crowd
  of PII "me too" boxes.  Expensive mass marketing probably wouldn't
  be effective on the class of buyers who need (or think they need) the
  extra performance of Alpha.

  BTW, it wouldn't take much NT market share for Alpha to move ahead of
  SPARC into second place in desktop RISC shipments (especially given
  the stagnation in the Unix workstation market NT is causing).  This
  would be a big moral victory for DEC while not materially affecting
  Intel enough to provoke a devastating pricing war.

> 
> Or will this PENTIUM ad campaign aimed at the public/TV not going to
> affect ALPHA's image and sales ?

  Most of the people Intel targets in their campaigns probably have never
  heard of Alpha.


