> Intel formed a joint gov/private enterprise to see if EUVL can be used to
> etch computer chips - because the current process (.25u is current
> production line best) works to certain level (about .3u???) then the
> electrons start hopping the paths with predictable resulting chaos.
> EUVL MAY allow for smaller pathways to etched with a new process.
> IF this process (developed for nuclear weapons manufacture) does eventually
> prove useful we'll see it in chips (YEARS from now) not in any form we'd
> probably recognize - unless Intel decides to beat the Pentium name to death
> EVEN more than it has. 8)
> Also Intel is joint partners with Motorola and others so they won't have
> exclusive access to this technology.
> 
> =tkk
This project is being undertaken because the wavelength of visible light
will in a few years time prevent chip makers from making denser chips,
not because the electrons will "hop" across paths.  In fact they will
most likely begin running into "electron hop" problems more quickly
using EUVL technology, as everything will be so much more closer
together.  They thought originally that this would happen at 1.0u, but
as someone once said "we went through the 1u (1 micron) barrier like it
wasn't there".  Turned out they could go quite a bit more.  I don't
think anyones is really sure at what level quantum effects will start to
appear in chip manufacturing, I guess they'll keep shrinking them till
they hit the wall:)

BTW, .3u is BIGGER than .25u (the smaller the number here, the better) 
They say current production techniques will allow them to get to about
.1u or so, but after that light wavelength limits them.

I think this technology won't be allowed JUST into the hands of Intel
etc.....  Everyone should benefit from it.

