>> On 12 Sep 1997 07:57:16 +0200, Tom I Helbekkmo <tih@nhh.no> wrote:
>> 
>> >Oh, don't worry.  By the time it's ready, Microsoft Windows will need
>> >everything it can deliver just to boot, and running Word will still be
>> >painfully slow.  Trust M$ to screw up anything they touch!
>> 
>> I don't know this for a fact, but my guess is that MS (and other
>> companies) makes their software slower on purpose in order to keep the
>> hardware companies in business.  People buy faster hardware to run the
>> new software at the same speed as the prior version then buy more
>> bloated software to run on the faster systems, then buy faster
>> hardware....
>
>I doubt if it's intentional. I personally think it stems from the way
>developers write code. When a customer asks for a new feature, it gets
>included whether it makes sense at all. Then, when they sit down to do the
>code, it's easier to add a new set of routines than to go back and work it
>into existing stuff. So you end up with thousands of unrelated routines
>that don't work well together. If there's a problem, you write a _new_
>routine instead of fixing the old ones. And so on.
>
>It's not the engineers' fault. It stems from Management's willingness to
>add every little feature that anyone might want and the short timeline
>available for modern software development.
>
>Look at the effort to put an entire Internet setup on a floppy disk (OS,
>Internet tools, and some content). When you write things from scratch with
>an intent to keep it lean, you'd be amazed what can be done.

(pull out soapbox)

Well, I think the users (actually the press) have more to do with it than
anything.  Better formatting, WYSIWYG, background spell checking,
scriptable word processors, grammer checking, ocr, speech recognition,
graphics processing, high resolution/color depth displays.

All of these things (and more) take an enormous amount of processing power.
If everyone had kept WP5.1, the faster hardware would have been useless.
Since everyone NEEDS these fratures apparently, they need a much faster
computer to keep the speed of the software with more features running as
fast as the software without the features, and this isn't even taking into
account the processing power required for games.

Management wouldn't ask the engineers to do it unless there was at least
some reason to do it, and most engineers don't just blindly follow stupid
ideas (some have no choice though).  Once one product has a feature, the
press focuses on that feature, consumers buy the product, and all of the
competetors need to add the feature to make it by the feature-by-feature
comparisons.

(put soapbox away)

Trust me, Microsoft spends a decent amount of time trying to make their
applications go as fast as possible given the constraints placed upon them.
The used to write a lot of the core routines in assembly (they may still)
just to get the extra little bit of speed out.

-Pat



