> 
> The DECmate III was (almost) the last in the long and illustrous line of
> PDP-8 machines. It was sold as a dedicated word processor, but in theory
> it can run any OS and software that other PDP-8's can. You'll want to
> check out alt.sys.pdp8 for this one.
> 
> Cheers,
> Sander
> --
> Sander van Malssen -- svm@kozmix.ow.nl -- http://www.cistron.nl/~svm/
>         * The 1-2-5 Page: http://www.cistron.nl/~svm/music/ *
Yep. At one time at least, you could get os/278 for a Decmate III
and run it. There was some grief because the DECUS library version
of the diskettes had "slushware" for Decmate II (also pdp8) and
needed this transferred from the WPS diskettes that generally came
with a Decmate III to let os/278 work. Once you had it, though,
it could access WPS diskettes and be used as a real machine. Several
compilers exist (or existed...might be hard to lay hands on them
if nobody's saved them)...os/278 came with an assembler or two,
and there was Pascal and there was Basic and DEC used to have a
Fortran for the thing. (If anyone started giving that away I can't
imagine that anyone at DEC would even care. In our town I've
seen Decmate II and III units at the dump.

The pdp8 ...any pdp8... is not a blindingly fast machine by modern
standards; rather the contrary. Emulators however could be pretty
reasonable. The pdp8 instruction set is rather simple, after all, 
and a considerable amount of skilled work went into programming
them for a number of years.



