kshuff wrote:
> 
>   Just picked up some surplus monitors and among them found a VAXmate and a
> DECmate III. Can anyone give me some specs on these two machines and what they
> would be used for. Both are operational, the DECmate is running some kind of
> word processor from the floppy (no hard drive installed), and the VAXmate is
> running a VT240 terminal emulator, also from floppy. Any help would be
> appreciated.

The VAXmate is an Intel 80286 CPU PC.

The so called "system unit" includes the motherboard, 80286 CPU,
1MB of memory, monitor & video controller, floppy drive & controller,
Ethernet controller (AMD LANCE -based; pretty much same as the
original 8-bit Digital DEPCA for "industry standard" PCs), serial
port, serial printer port, mouse port, speaker and power supply.

Optionally it could include an expansion box (so called "pizza box")
that attached to the bottom of the system unit. The expansion
box contained an additional power supply, a battery and a 20MB
hard drive & controller + two 16-bit ISA slots.

Other options were a 80287 coprocessor, a 2MB memory expansion
module and a 2400 bps (U.S.) modem. All installable in the
system unit.

Its video controller is compatible with CGA and it can handle
"industry standard" text modes (of the '86 era). It also has
a higher resolution mode (800x252 + a couple of 640x400 "Digital 
extended" modes), which the VAXmate-specific VT240 emulator 
(SETHOST.EXE - note that there's also a text-only SETHOST which 
has been part of DECnet-DOS, PCSA/PATHWORKS through the years) 
could utilize.

There was also support for Microsoft Windows; when the
VAXmate appeared, Windows 1.03 was current, then it was
upgraded to V2.0, if my memory serves me right.

The core OS supported was MS-DOS V3.1 with an upgrade to V3.3.
The OEM version also included additional utilities such as
(LK250) keyboard drivers, printer drivers, serial port (XON/XOFF)
driver, FDISK, etc.

I've also run PC-DOS 3/4 and MS-DOS 5.0/6.0 on a VAXmate.

More or less, it operates as any 286 PC, but with a Digital
keyboard and non-standard (standard for text and CGA modes)
video, built in networking and no parallel printer port.

FWIW, there was also a hack/patch to the Compaq plasma 
display (Compaq Convertible I or II) driver (which was
essentially CGA, even if Microsoft dropped CGA support
from Windows 3.0) for Windows 3.0 that enabled one to 
run Windows 3.0 on the VAXmate.

