Philip Nasadowski wrote:
> 
> Hi, I'm just a college student, and prettyu new to Vaxes in general, and
> I've now got a VaxStation II (it followed me home, honest :) which I'm
> trying to breathe life into.  Things are going good, however, research on
> campus has indicated that this particular machine has unreliable hard
> drives.  Looking inside, the drives look to the PC-AT (MFM) type.  They
> have the two cables running from the signal distribution board (?) to the
> drives.

That "signal distribution board" is the CPU!

> Actually, none of them have any DEC markings on them, and two
> look like they were intended for PCs (they have a black front plate (full
> height) and drive lights.).

Some of the drives DEC relabled had front covers with lights.  You
probably have an RD53 (which was a Quantum or a Micropolis) at ~70MB, or
an RD54 (a slightly modified Maxtor XT2190) at ~150MB.  The Maxtors
almost always had the front covers.

> My question is this:
> 
> The only manuals I have don't say anything about the actual drive
> interface.  Are these really MFM drives, and if so, can I use any drive
> out of an old PC.  If not, what kind of bus is this, and where can I find
> drives for it.
> 
> Thanks!! :)

Yes, they are MFM, using an interface commonly known as ST506.  You can
try to use other ST506 drives, but I believe (and I'm sure someone will
correct me) that it only supports the drives that DEC sold.  Used
XT2190s can be had for around $100.00 if you look hard enough.

Bear in mind, though, that at 150MB, an RD54 or XT2190 is no longer
supported as a VMS system device.  You have to do some careful tailoring
to get it to fit, and even then, you have very little space left for
anything else.

Good luck!

--

