Correct, they are indeed ntothing more than ordinary MFM drives,
however most microVAXen will not allow you to use just any old
MFM drive.
The geometry for the RD52, RD53, and RD54 are known to the controller, 
but for other drives it not. On the VAXstation 2000 you can enter
novel and interesting disk geometries for other disks, ergo things you
can get from the PC world, but you need a *ton* of detailed information
about the thing, not just CHS and landing zones, but timings and all kinds
of crud like that. If you can get info like that, you have a chance of 
convincing the mahcien to understand a non-RD type drive. 

I think in the microVAX FAQ there's some info on actually telling the system
about disk geometry. I dont remember offhand. I also dont think the RQDX3 can
handle or recognize disks that are not an RD5{2||3||4}. (You call your box a
vaxstation II, are you sure its nto a vaxstation 2000? or a microvax II?)

anwyays, good luck with it!

isildur



