Henryk Marciniak wrote:

> Hello VAX guru's.
> Could someone explain me how to make VAX/VMS 5.5 recognize a non-Digital
> SCSI disk?
> The disk is IBM DSAS-3540 548MB, 3 heads, 1070496 blocks. The machine is
> VAXstation 4000-60.
> I went as far as erasing the disk with the SCSI utility available from
> the console prompt (TEST/UTIL SCSI). It went fine, therefore I assume
> the disk is functioning correctly. Show device/full command says: device
> type Generic SCSI disk.  But both, initialize and mount DCL commands
> say: "fatal disk error". I guest I must tell the system the number of

> heads, cyllinders, tracks etc. but have no idea how to do that.

No, it is not necessary to tell the system the drive geometry. (Nor can
you.)

VMS V5.5 can be somewhat picky about the SCSI attributes of the drives it
supports.  In particular, if the drive has the ARRE or AWRE bit set, you
will get the "fatal disk error" message.

I would start by installing the latest VAXSCSIxxx patch kit.  If that
doesn't fix it, you have 3 options:

1) Get a different disk drive.
2) Upgrade to a later version of VMS.
3) Rewrite the SCSI mode bits on the drive to be compatible.

Good luck!


BTW: If you are really running VMS V5.5, I would strongly recommend
upgrading to at least V5.5-2.

