In article <5vac8b$k8p@bgtnsc02.worldnet.att.net>,
Mark E. Levy <LEVY.M@ACM.ORG> wrote:
>Cheap software may be inexpensive initially, but costs more in the long
>run.
>  
>> But what if it costs less for a company to but the $49.95 software and
>> hire you for a few days to get it working compared to buying the $5000
>> Digital software and hire you for a few hours to get it working ?
>
>Because the $5,000 software will still be working next week, or next
>year...

That's part of the problem.  Microsoft has dominated the market through
creating a cultural rather than technological change.

People I know who are running NT servers have simply incorporated
hitting the reset button into their daily routine.  To me this is
spooky. 

Have you ever noticed that even on the client side, network related
error messages in Win 95 say "server not responding?"  If they aren't
even connected, they get a message saying that there's something wrong
with their DNS server.  I believe that this is a deliberate marketing
strategy directed against "the zero downtime mentality."  Users are
being conditioned to accept less and less reliabily, and to distribute
blame to some amorphous vaguely understood concept of "server."  This
will create some user expectation slack that NT will readily take up.

You can't seriously expect a web server to just keep running, day after
day...  can you?


- Ari

