Curt Sampson wrote:

> My
> claim is that under some circumstances it's cheaper, or better, or
> both, to do your own OS support.

Which is precisely what I disagree with.

> >My position is that I've solved problems for 20 years without OS
> >mods....
> 
> An essentially meaningless position. I've never needed VMS to solve
> a computing problem, that does not mean that there are not problems
> out there for which it is the best solution.

And I've never needed any kind of Unix to solve a computing problem,
which is why I find OS mods unnecessary.
 
What we're arguing about here is basic OS culture.  The Unix culture has
been to roll your own as much as is desired.  Many of the early Unixes
were always supplied with the source code, and modifying it was
encouraged.

I grew up using commercial OSs, where source code was difficult or
impossible to come by.  Making kernel changes was universally accepted
as a bad idea.  I still hold to that philosophy since I support
commercial OSs, which, in any case, I've had no need to modify, even if
the means were available.  The rare occasion I've needed to do something
that was not part of the OS was easy to implement with a kernel mode
routine that hooks itself in only when needed.  The base OS was not
changed.

I stand by my position, irrespective of whether or not you agree with
it.

--

