In article <3417F258.11873CFF@rdc.nl>, Laura Hannema <hannema@rdc.nl> writes:
:I�ve got a two problems with proxy�s between a  VMS 6.2 ALPHA system and
:a VMS 5.5-2 VAX system,
:and also between two VMS 6.2 ALPHA systems.
:It works oke between two VMS 5.5-2 VAX systems.
:
:In the proxy command  I use the same username.

  What was the proxy command used?

  What was the DCL command used?
  What remote accesses worked?
	(and from what node to what node)?
  What remote accesses logged in under the correct remote username?
	(and from what node to what node)?

:My first problem is that sometimes  the sys$login: directory is the
:sys$specific directory.

  On the same system with unchanged proxies, an inconsistent set of
  target directories would tend to point to a serious problem in the
  proxy processing, to the use of different commands, to the use of
  different proxies, or to some site-specific SET DEFAULT code in the
  system-wide SYLOGIN or user-specific LOGIN.

:My second problem is that the proxy doesn�t work at all.

  Please define "doesn't work".  (That particular phrase is the bane
  of technical support. :-)  Do you get any error messages?  Have
  you enabled and checked for any login security alarms?  Have you
  checked for NETSERVER.LOG files in the intended default directory
  for the login?  In the DECnet or FAL default directory?

:Does sombody  know what I can try to let the systems talk to each other
:with proxy-accounts.

  Have you confirmed that each node has all local nodenames in the
  local DECnet database?  (If DECnet doesn't know how to map the
  node address to the node name, then OpenVMS LOGINOUT doesn't know
  that a particular remote access matches a particular proxy.)

