b.vanvalkenburg wrote:
> 
> The decmate is a word processor, just as you've discovered.
> The vaxmate is a IBM terminal emulator (but can be used as a real dumb pc)  that
> Digital made to satisfy a market that didn't care what it costed as long as it
> was Digital.
> 

Actually, the VAXmate was an AT-compatible PC that DEC brought out in
September 1986. The VAXmate's claim to fame was integrated Ethernet and
VMS Services for MS-DOS, software that allowed VAXmate users to store
data in "container files" on VAX/VMS systems.

The VAXmate was tres pricey: right around $6K. The joke at the time was
that you could buy an AT-compatible from DEC for $6K, or you could buy
an AT-compatible from Hyundai for $6K. The difference was that Hyundai
included an automobile in the purchase price.

The VAXmate wasn't exactly one of DEC's stellar successes, but the VMS
Services for MS-DOS was a progenitor of Pathworks, and of the
recently-introduced WNT disk services for VMS.

