Actually, I just thought of a better example.

I company I did some work for last year has an automated warehouse
picking system run by a MicroVax 4000/105.  This is a 24hour/7day
real-time system.  Interfaced to it are radio-frequency terminals
attached to forklifts, ink-jet printers that shoot the delivery address
onto boxes as they pass by on the conveyor, bar-code readers that read
moving boxes, etc.  The system would read the bar codes and kick the box
off the conveyor to the appropriate truck, and print a bill of lading
when it was full.  Orders would be downloaded from the mainframe to be
picked, and completed shipments would be uploaded.  This system would
also receive finished goods from the factory and store them in the
warehouse.

In the 10 years or so they've been using this system, outages have
primarily been caused by programmer carelessnes.  All of the disks are
shadowed to prevent drive failures from causing problems.  All of the
disks are also cabled to a second 4000/105 which serves as the
development system.  It could be brought up in place of the production
system in a few minutes if necessary.  No one there remembers ever
having to do that.  The CPUs (and memory) have never failed, from the
original 6000 class machines through the conversion to the 4000s, and
VMS has never crashed.

I don't recall how much this company said downtime costs, but it was a
considerable figure, and accounts for hundreds of people sitting around,
no product shipping, etc.  It was cheaper for their mainframe to be
down.

Recalling the screen displays, I don't see any way that a GUI would have
helped.

How would a Micro$h*t "upgrade" help here?

Oh, and in case anyone is wondering, the O/S has not been modified. 
It's stock, right off the CD.

