I'm including a short report I wrote regarding the various C compilers
for Dec Alphas running DU 4.0.  The hydrocode referred to is a
one-dimensional code which models supernova explosions--it's nearly
all floating point arithmatic and pointer manipulations.

\documentstyle[12pt]{article}

\addtolength{\textheight}{1in}
\setlength{\voffset}{-0.25in}
\addtolength{\textwidth}{1in}
\setlength{\hoffset}{-0.5in}

\author{Randall Smith//rsmith@stars.gsfc.nasa.gov}
\title{Tests of compiler speed}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

A short project to determine what compilers and settings should be
used on the DEC Alpha.  We have OSF/1 v3.2 cc, and GNU gcc v2.6.3.  I
used the file test01.inp (given in Appendix 1) as input to the
hydrocode, and the program t\_malloc.c (Appendix 2). 

All runs gave identical results, as expected.  Some runs were done
twice, to get a feel for the random variation in the results and to
check results.  The winner was the OSF c compiler, with maximal
optimization turned on.  Coming in at 2\% slower was gcc with
maximum optimization and debugging information.  Oddly enough, running
gcc at full optimization but without debugging resulted in a code
running even slower.

The results for the hydrocode are shown on the next page, both as a
summary and the raw results.  

The t\_malloc program, designed to test only memory allocation, was run
only with --O3 optimization on each compiler.  The program has three
cases: arrays, pointers allocated in a single space, and pointers
allocated semi-randomly, with intervening space.  The results are
tabulated below, and show that if arrays can be used, there is a clear
10\% benefit at least.  If pointers must be used, then grouping them
does not give a clear benefit. \footnote{Units are in clock ticks on a
DEC 3000/300LX} 
