> hardware companies in business.  People buy faster hardware to run the
> new software at the same speed as the prior version then buy more
> bloated software to run on the faster systems, then buy faster
> hardware....

I doubt if it's intentional. I personally think it stems from the way
developers write code. When a customer asks for a new feature, it gets
included whether it makes sense at all. Then, when they sit down to do the
code, it's easier to add a new set of routines than to go back and work it
into existing stuff. So you end up with thousands of unrelated routines
that don't work well together. If there's a problem, you write a _new_
routine instead of fixing the old ones. And so on.

It's not the engineers' fault. It stems from Management's willingness to
add every little feature that anyone might want and the short timeline
available for modern software development.

Look at the effort to put an entire Internet setup on a floppy disk (OS,
Internet tools, and some content). When you write things from scratch with
an intent to keep it lean, you'd be amazed what can be done.

