I'll put this in the simplest possible terms.  I support OpenVMS.  I do
not work with, support, or use in any way any form of Unix.

OpenVMS is sufficiently extendable in supported ways that kernel O/S
mods are not necessary.  It was _designed_ that way.  In any case, the
source code is not available without a source license, which is a
multi-megabuck purchase.

I haven't looked into it, but I don't suspect that Micro$oft makes their
source code available cheaply either, if at all.

I don't consider an O/S that can't do the job without mods to be a
prudent choice.  Would you buy a compact car to haul bricks?  Does it
make sense to buy a compact car with the intention of modifying it so
that it can haul bricks?  Sure, it could be done, but in the end, you
still have a modified compact car, and the total cost of the car plus
modifications probably cost more than the truck you should have bought
in the first place.  If your modified compact car breaks, you fix it. 
You can take the truck back for warranty service.

I'll take the truck with an extended warranty, thank you.  Working with
computers of any type is aggravating enough without voluntarily adding
to it.

--

