Mark E. Levy wrote:
> And I've already said that a company I worked for put the cost of
> downtime at $20,000/hour.  The statement above was in reference to a
> comment that a different system could SAVE a company $30,000/hour.
> That's a little hard to believe.

I have managed a system where downtime for 2 hours at a particular time
of the day would cost $100,000 (ok, 72,000 US dollars). There were two
such periods in the day. And yes, it was on VMS, and yes, that
application was important enough to justify a disaster recovery. As a
matter of fact, this app was the first TRULY "disaster proofed" on at
that bank, well before ATMs and the rest (which proved too expensive to
disaster-proof and their platforms (Tandem and IBM) did not at that time
have the capability to do disk shadowing across buildings).

But the point I was trying to make.

If you have a central server that served so many people that a downtime
of an hour costs $20k in lost productivity (eg: affects a LOT of
people), then it is possible that it is a "legacy" architecture,
character cell etc.

If you migrate to  Microstuff stuff, you'll get the end-user
productivity gains due to GUI (in many cases) and distributed computing
and these productivity gains, in a large organisation, may well be worth
the extra downtime they will get out of the game-quality software.

In other words: if you move to a PC which has software that allows you
do do in 20 minutes what took an hour to do on your old robust system,
then the PC can afford to be down 40 minutes each hour and you still end
up doing the same amount of work. And since PC stuff costs a lot less
initially, you end up a winner.

Of course, I am speculating on the values here, but I think that there
are cases where this type of logic may apply.



