X-Deja-AN: 271899131
References: <te4t7qoiiq.fsf@grandteton.cs.uiuc.edu>
Organization: University of Michigan, Electrical Engineering and Computer Science
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.arch


In a previous article,  Arun Sharma  <arun.sharma_no_spam@uiuc.edu> wrote:
>The Alpha architecture reference manual says that accessing memory
>using physical addresses could be slower (while servicing TB misses
>in PAL code) than mapping the page tables and accessing them using
>virtual addresses.
>
>Can anyone explain why ?

I'm not sure about specific alpha implementations, but there are a
number of possiblities.  Most have to do with the fact that it's
better to optimize for virtual accesses, since that what you do the
most.

One possible scenario is a virtually addressed first level cache.  If
all physical accesses have to be sent to L2, you would see a
performance hit.

Another may be pipline interlocks that check for read after write, but
only check in virtual address space, so the pipe might require
draining between memory accesses.

Whether the Alpha implementations exhibit these specific situations, I
do not know.  There are certainly other memory access patterns that
could thrash the TB and tilt the comparison the other way.

-Jeff Bell




