In article <5vacf4$463$3@nntp2.ba.best.com>,
	jim@jimbrooks.NOSPAM.org (Jim Brooks) writes:
   Intel plans to fab PIV using a new process technology called
   "Extreme Ultra Violet Lithography" (whatever that means, I don't
   know squat about hardcore EE crap.  I'm just parroting what I read,
   Virginia).  This new process, in addition to yielding
   ultrasuperfast nanoprocessors, allows 1000 times the memory
   density.  So we will soon be upgrading our memory in units of
   gigabytes (Mr. Nudds, are you there)?

I'm not sure what's impressive about this, though.  It's almost as
inevitable as the tides.  The article says 2011 for 100BIPS (Billion
Instructions Per Second - or should it be GIPS?).  Since
not-so-current chips (IE, PPro 200) crank something like 400 VAX MIPS,
and that's 14 years off, and speeds are supposed to double every two
years, we should expect the PPro200-level chip of 2011 to run at
40BIPS.  40BIPS, 100BIPS, what's the difference, it's not like PPro200
is the top-of-the-line for current chips, anyhow.

   Since this PIV will be totally overkill, I wonder what scheme Intel
   will devise to convince us we must buy a PV?

Depends.  After all, we're forecasting forward 14 years, which is
almost as long as the person computer industry has _existed_, and
about half as long as computers have been in widespread commercial
use.  Someone using their Altair in 1975 probably wouldn't have much
of any idea what they could do with a CPU 1000x as fast, with 100,000x
as much memory.  Fortunately, we have many people working diligently
on wasting^H^H^H^H^H^H^Husing the extra capacity.  Word2011 will
probably not only complain about your writing style, it will also
complain about the subject matter and the lack of intelligence of your
readers.  That's assuming that our minds haven't all gone to mush due
to video-on-demand, and everybody has forgotten how to read.  In that
case, perhaps it will be a word process for video that we'll be
using:-).

Later,
