In article <01bcb7fc$44bb04c0$1d49cf8d@wardch>, ccw@idt.nj says...
>

>  The Falcon
>was a single chip only sold as a controller (well, maybe they put it on a
>Q-BUS).  The 11/23 was the new LSI-11 chip set, and the 11/24 was the
>Unibus version.  No personal experience on the rest....
>
The single chip you refer to was the T11 chip, which was used on the Falcon,
DEUNAs disk controllers and other DEC peripherals from the 1982 onwards era.

The Falcon was a fully functional SBC, with parallel ports, two serial, which
could run from ROM or RAM. It would run RT11, initially with a patch from
a DECUS contributor, but in later RT11 versions 5.x could be built for.

MACDBG, a Macro-11 target development system, could be used to download macro
code from RT11 to a target Falcon with the KXT11-BA DEC proms installed.

A company called Infosphere developed a STOIC (FORTH) like environment for the
Falcon. I have one functional here. With that, the Falcon will function as
a fully functional unit, supporting TU58, RL0x, and RX02 peripherals in a back
plane, but without mass storage still functional from ROM with a VT100 Forth
editor. The O/S was fully multitasking, with maths, transcendental 
functions, bit operators et al.

Postitronika in the Netherlands, with Ernst Lopez-Cardozo, put a boot prom on
Falcons, to operate with their Techlan token passing 2.5mb network, which 
would allow a Falcon to run from a RT11 server. It worked very well.

The later -B series Falcons had different mapping, and clock circuitry and 
generally used to stuff code up written for the KXT11-AA series Falcons which 
is why the later command a  premium on the second hand market.


Ken Kirkby




