Laura Hannema wrote:
> 
> I�ve got a two problems with proxy�s between a  VMS 6.2 ALPHA system and
> a VMS 5.5-2 VAX system,
> and also between two VMS 6.2 ALPHA systems.
> It works oke between two VMS 5.5-2 VAX systems.
> 
> In the proxy command  I use the same username.
> 
> My first problem is that sometimes  the sys$login: directory is the
> sys$specific directory.
> My second problem is that the proxy doesn�t work at all.
> 
> Does sombody  know what I can try to let the systems talk to each other
> with proxy-accounts.
> 
> Laura Hannema

The easiest answer is to start with the opcom messages on each machine.
Assume that NODEA1 and NODEA2 are the alphas and NODEV1 is the vax.

NODEA1$ REPLY/TEMP/ENABLE
NODEA2$ REPLY/TEMP/ENABLE
NODEV1$ REPLY/TEMP/ENABLE

NODEA1$ DIRECTORY NODEA1::	!
NODEA2$ DIRECTORY NODEA1::	! Look at NODEA1's opcom
NODEV1$ DIRECTORY NODEA1::	!

NODEA1$ DIRECTORY NODEA2::	!
NODEA2$ DIRECTORY NODEA2::	! Look at NODEA2's opcom
NODEV1$ DIRECTORY NODEA2::	! 

NODEA1$ DIRECTORY NODEV1::	!
NODEA2$ DIRECTORY NODEV1::	! Look at NODEV1's opcom
NODEV1$ DIRECTORY NODEV1::	!

Look at the opcom messages noting the remote-node, remote-user,
local-user.
Then look at the local-user authorization record and note the default
device.

In the case of "login information invalid at remote node", look at the
proxy on the remote node and then the local-user authorization record.

Proxies take the form of remote-node::remote-user
[other-local-user,]local-user/DEFAULT

If none of this helps, RUN SYS$SYSTEM:CONVERT_PROXIES on NODEA1 and
NODEA2
and maybe run MCR NCP SET KNOWN PROXIES ALL on NODEA1 and NODEA2



