Mark E. Levy wrote:
> I'm not talking about commodity PC software here.  The warranties of the
> software I buy contains no such verbiage.

OK, lets talk about "Digital" software.

A few years ago, I bought Message Router for for home vax (bundled with
ALL-IN-1). The release notes specified that one of the documented
functions did not work and this function was important to deliver
message as soon as they are received by MR.

I called DEC to get the patch which is what the release notes said I
should do.

BUT, Digital Canada said that because I did not have *VMS* support, I
was not allowed to get the patches and I would have to pay a minimum of
2 hours support for someone to prepare the tape for me. 

Remember, Digital admitted IN THE RELEASE NOTES, PART OF THE
DISTRIBUTION, that there was a bug. And DEC was unwilling to give me
that patch. (I finally did get it, but have to raise my voice quite a
bit to get it).

On the other hand, I often get emails from some mac software vendors
(Macromedia) telling me about new patches available for free downloads
at their web site (bug fixes and corrections).

Another example ?

I ordered the whole VMS-SNA interface kit with 2 DEMSAs (for
redundancy). DIGITAL insisted that we pay for DEC consulting to do the
installation.
The DEC guy was a good VMS guru and was able to quickly fix the
VMSINSTAL bug which prevented the VMS software from installing but knew
very little about 
the MVS side of installation.

BUT WORSE, he didn't do his homework and also mention that there was an
important patch required on the MVS software. You see, without that
patch,
there was NO security on the MVS server software and from any VMS
account,
you could delete *ALL* datasets on the IBM mainframe.  I had to get DEC
in the USA to download the patch to my own VAX at home, put it on a
TK50, brought it to the VAX at the bank, copied it to magtape and then
had it installed BY OUR OWN STAFF at the bank on the mainframe. Service
you said ?????



Sorry, but some "large established" vendors often have difficulty with
customer service.

I do not know if Digital-Canada still has that policy of not providing
ANY service (even when on warrantee) to layered software unless there is
a service contract on VMS, but Digital has a LONG way to go to get VMS
ownership to be similarly priced to PC software.

I also do not know how much things have changed, but back then,
upgrading VMS on a MicrovaxII would have cost me about CAD $5000.00 when
an upgrade on a MAC cost about $100 (actually, for a long time, it was
free). Upgrades for 
MAC software such as Freehand,Photoshop etc is about USD$150 per year
with a certain period of free technical support. (30to 90 days).

When you compare the cost of DEC software against that of PC/MAC
software, you can hire a consultant for quite a few hours to get the
PC/MAC software running smoothly and still end up ahead of DEC software,
especially if the later has bugs and you need to fight with DEC to get
patches.

Worse yet, I tried REPORTING bugs on a *PC* software package called
TEAMLINKS.
Digital Canada, since I did not have *VMS* support, wanted me to pay
$200 (minimum one hour support) to REPORT THE BUG. At the time, I was an
ISV and the
ALL-IN-1 engineers to whom I had access, did not want to handle my
report directly and insisted I go through the right channels. I was
lucky that another customer reported the bug months later and DEC
finally beleived there was a bug there.

Sorry, but there are 2 sides of all coins here. Some customers may be
very happy with Digital, (usually a few large/key customers) but I think
that many smaller customers may be wondering if DEC is worth the extra
money.



