> Intel plans to fab PIV using a new process technology called
> "Extreme Ultra Violet Lithography" (whatever that means,
> I don't know squat about hardcore EE crap. 

Extreme UltraViolet refers to EM Waves with a wavelength of
approximately 2 to 4 nanometers (2-4 x 10^-9 meters) in wavelength. 
Compare this to current technology which is at about the .25
micron (micrometer) size (2.5 x 10-7 meters).

In other words, they are talking about shrinking down the size of
the circuitry by a factor of approximately 100!!! Yikes and egad.

Lithography is simply using a pattern to replicate features....

So, what you've got is a beam of EUV used to create the chips.

Of course to do this, they've got to reinvent just about everything
in the process.

Currently, if memory serves me correctly, they're still using
either visible light, or "near" ultraviolet.

Anyway, I'm not in EE or a physicist, but I do play one at work
from time to time.

