Niclas Mattsson wrote:
> I'm trying to figure out a suitable configuration for a new Personal
> Workstation. My main application is nonlinear optimization with very
> large problem sizes. The question is: will the extra 2 MB cache help > me?

If your code (instruction and data streams) tends to "hit" a lot in a
two MByte region of memory, then the optional Bcache will help.  How
much it helps depends on the relative hit rates.  You're the best judge
of the memory access patterns of your own code;  if you're using a
commercial software package, then you'd have to tell us which one, and
specify the size of the problem set, before anyone could give you
reasonable estimates.

Main memory is pretty fast on the Alpha-based DIGITAL Personal
Workstations.  It's actually faster when there is no Bcache in the way,
so if your main application can't take advantage of the Bcache then it's
better not to have one.

> And is there any difference between 433a, 500a and 600a other than
> processor speed?

The 600a Main Logic Board has a beefier voltage regulator to handle the
faster CPU (although we may have moved all systems to that MLB to
simplify inventory).  Because of clock divide ratios and other
esoterica, the system bus is a bit faster on the 600 MHz system, which
speeds up the main memory, Bcache and PCI bus a bit.

