I would say that my 20 years of not having the problems you describe
speak for thenselves.

> So what is your point here? Is it that nobody should run Unix or
> PC software, and the entire world should switch to VMS? Do you
> honestly think that VMS running on Digital hardware is the best
> and most economical solution to every computing problem currently
> known to man?

I would not presume to say that VMS fits everywhere, but it certainly
fits in many of the places now occupied by PCs and Unixes.
 
> If not, and since you admit that, for example, many PC vendors
> provide complete crap in the way of software and support, do you
> still think it's not possible, and posibly even a better thing,
> for some companies in some situations to do their own development
> and support?

So what you're saying is that we should just accept that crap software
and just fix it ourselves.  No, I prefer to not use it.  If more people
had that attitude, crap software would not be profitable.

> At this point, I'm a little mystified as to what your argument
> exactly is. It appears to be `free software with in-house support
> is never an appropriate computing solution.' But every time someone
> comes up with a statement showing how sometimes vendors fall down,
> you just say `well, I don't have that problem.' You're not supporting
> your argument very well, or at all, or you're not making it clear
> just what you're trying to say.

Well, I don't have that problem.  When what I'm doing obviously works
well, I fail to see why I should use something that will take MORE of my
available time to achieve the same result.  I'd much rather play with my
kids than hack the O/S, thank you.
 
> >What's happening with PCs and Unix today is not representative of the
> >state of the computer industry elsewhere.
> 
> No, but what's happening with PCs and Unix today is representative
> of the majority of what users see when they work with computers.

So Micro$loth, Intel and other PC software and hardware vendors, and
Unixes, have lowered industry standards, and we should just live with
it, right?  Instead of forcing the vendors to produce quality software
and provide adequate support, we should just do it ourselves, right? 
Simply because many, perhaps even most, people are willing to accept
that doesn't mean I will.  There are those of us who know what real
robust systems are which require a minimum of effort to maintain (yes,
these are typically called "proprietary" systems) and refuse to accept
today's trendy shoveware (sorry Paddy).

I'll close this whole thing with this anecdote, culled from my own
personal history.  Years ago, I worked for a national lab.  That lab was
primarily VMS based.  I was a member of a five-person group with
responsibility for supporting 400+ VMS systems.  One 100-node VMS
cluster (in another dept.) was managed by a single person.  Unix systems
began to appear, and after awhile, their numbers approached parity with
the VMS systems.  The Unix support group had ~15 people, who worked like
dogs.  We, of the VMS group, had plenty of spare time on our hands. 
Certainly, some of this may have been due to the fact that the VMS
systems were "mature" and the Unix systems were new, but the VMS support
group was never as large or worked as hard as the Unix group did, even
at a similiar point in the growth cycle.

We never attempted or desired to modify the O/S.  We did have problems,
which was due in large part to the fact that we usually stressed things
past the design limit.  We got prompt response to phone calls, typically
not having to wait on hold for more than three minutes, after which we
would get someone who was familiar with our installation. Problems sent
via e-mail (which were not of a critical nature) would elicit a response
the same day.

I'll leave you to draw your own conclusions.
