In article <34089e4d.2459055@nntp.netcomuk.co.uk>, rstevens@netcomuk.co.uk (Rob) writes:
:Yes, I thought that was a particularly condescending (but not
:unexpected) comment from Mr Hoffman, 

  If you feel my comments were condescending, direct email with
  constructive comments, flames :-), or suggestions are welcome.

:...especially as he is not in
:possession of the facts about my installation. The entire cluster was
:shut down daily for security reasons (unfortunately), and the two Vax
:4000's were actually very loosely tied to the AS2100 as they have the
:sole purpose of capturing high speed data from various sources, for
:feed to the other parts of the network.

  I'm quite familiar with military security -- both the theoretical
  mandatory and discretionary access control aspects, and the more
  practical aspects.  (Uh, yes, those rifles really are loaded.  :-)

  And I believe I understand what your site is up to, and I believe
  I understand the risks you are taking with your data.

  What I want to make sure is that you understand those risks...

..

:The quorum values were originally set up so that the capture machines
:could continue to work, even if the main systems were not, though
:failure.  The requirement for this has now changed though, and I
:decided, one evening, to ask you guys/gals what you thought about the
:situation I had come up against, especially as I'd been stupid and
:done my sums wrong. 

  EXPECTED_VOTES is not QUORUM -- we removed the QUORUM SYSGEN parameter
  a while back, and replaced it with EXPECTED_VOTES -- we made this change
  as we found customers were not setting QUORUM to the correct value, we
  determined we could implement a way to set the "live" quorum value to
  the correct value automatically, and we believed this would help reduce
  the risk of customer data corruptions.  (I may be repeating comments
  made in an earlier post here.)

  EXPECTED_VOTES is used to set the initial value of quorum -- if the value
  in EXPECTED_VOTES is incorrect, then the connection manager will notice
  and correct the setting -- here's the important part -- once the initial
  connections are established.  (I've mentioned that before.)  Once the
  connections are established, the running value of quorum is corrected.
  (It's this window before the connections are fully established when the
  data can be at risk.)

:                     I was not looking for the stuff, I had read in the
:manuals, Mr Hoffman.

  No offense is intended.  I'd not want to see your data scrozzled, and
  I'd not want to provide you with any suggestions that might lead you to
  unwittingly scrozzle your data.  (Never offend somebody that calls you
  "Mr" -- but I digress. :-)

  More than just the obvious coordination of shared disk storage is involved
  in the quorum mechanism.  Even though you might think you have a VMScluster
  with no shared storage, realize that each node has a picture of the state
  of the storage servers across the VMScluster, each node can have parts of
  the distributed lock tree, and each node can have cached data intended for
  storage on other nodes.

  The following is derived from a recent email exchange on this topic:

  The quorum scheme is a set of "blade guards" -- a construct that is
  designed to prevent your data from being severely trashed -- remove
  the "blade guards" at your own (great) peril.

  With a VMScluster with:

        o no shared SCSI interconnects,

        o no multi-host KFQSA connections,

        o no multi-host CI, MC, or DSSI interconnects,

        o no possibility that anyone will ever configure
          and begin to use shared interconnects....

  ... Then no, your data is probably not at any great risk of corruption.
  (And note that I did *not* say "no risk".)

  It is that last bullet that causes most system managers concern: you
  fall ill (or your trip to the DECUS symposium gets approved :-), and
  your second-in-command tries to get the new hardware configuration
  working because your boss needs it for The New Hot Project, and your
  stand-in doesn't know to check for the EXPECTED_VOTES "armed bomb"
  left lurking in SYSGEN...

  Should this "no shared interconnects" state of affairs change next week,
  next month, or next year, then yes, an incorrect setting in EXPECTED_VOTES
  can cause your data can become severely corrupted.  And the speed at which
  the data corruption will arise in a partitioned VMScluster will be quite
  surprising -- the last time I saw this happen, the system disk was quite
  scrozzled before anyone ever got to the "Username:" prompt...

  And even in a no-shared-storage VMScluster, data structures such as the
  lock manager are shared -- these structures reflect the configuration of
  each node.  And the lock manager can potentially get confused...

  And -- if you think about what it entails -- something as simple as
  booting off the wrong system root on a SCSI disk in a VMScluster with
  SCSI interconnects can trigger the EXPECTED_VOTES "bomb"...

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
