>     It seems that a partitioned cluster is only a danger if one has a resource
> (disk) which can be independantly accessed by more than one host and it is
> possible for the hosts to lose communication with each other without also losing
> connection to the disk. This was certainly possible in the CI/Star Coupler/HSC
> world. I believe it is also possible in a SCSI cluster ( the cluster management
> traffic taking a different path than the disk accesses ). 
> 
>     Perhaps I'm being dense here, but it appears to me that this is not a concern
> in NI based clusters. While it is easy to conceive of network failures which may cause
> a single cluster to split into 2 ( or more ), any resource available within one
> of those clusters would be safe as it would only be accesible to hosts which were
> fully participating in the cluster.

On the contrary.  Any process, procedure or device whose activity is
coordinated on two nodes by the lock manager can be corrupted by two instances
of the cluster.  Two that I can think of offhand immediately are a server
process that uses the "dead man's switch" type of lock to ensure that there
there is only one "master" process in a cluster with a process on each node
(there could be one master per cluster) and shadowing.  For shadowing, consider
a 2 member cluster with a 2 member shadowset, with each shadowed disk directly
attached to a cluster member and not accessible to the other member except
through the MSCP server.  Shut down the cluster and bring the systems up
as 2 1-node clusters.  The nodes will mount the shadowsets which come up
as single member sets since the other disk doesn't exist, as far as it is
concerned.  The disks are updated independently of each other, and now can't
be merged.  (if you bring the cluster up again as a 2 member cluster one
disk will be overwritten by the other - losing all work done to it)
For the "dead man's switch" example consider a server process that normally
produces a single log file which has entries that must be in timewise order.
You'll wind up with multiple log files that can't be appended in any order,
you'd have to do something to merge them.

-Mike



