Richard Bemrose <rb237@phy.cam.ac.uk> writes:

>I did not make it clear in my original post but 'ourmachine' is the
>hostname of the system I am currently setting up and not the servers
>hostname. That's why I am confused with the error message as I have not
>configured the local machine to be a NFS server.

But you sort of have.  Automount includes a small NFS V2 server.  When
it starts it does NFS mounts on the paths it intercepts.  The mount
call includes the server's hostname and port number, so automount
can do this much to people's amazement, confusion, and consternation.
You can see the intercept points with "mount -e".

>> > What is going wrong?

Usually when automount is having trouble like that some other protocol
is involved.  DNS name resolution can be the culprit - it can often
take a minute for a name lookup to fail.  During that time, anyone else
who queries automount is blocked.  They timeout, but then wait for the
RPC buffer to free.  On loopback mounts, it's sitting on automount's
incoming socket and once automount picks it up the application resumes,
retransmits, and succeeds immediately - hence the OK message immediately
after the not responding message.

If you're really careful, and don't mind risking hanging your system a few
times, try killing automount (-TERM or -INTR, ***never*** -KILL!)
and start it in a spare xterm with options -T -T -v.  Every access to
the automount server will be reported and should make it easy to diagnose.
You will probably be astounded at the volume of diagnostics, but they
are informative.
