Ronnen (ronnen@rbs.org) wrote:

: I picked up a DEC 3000/400 that had been discarded by a used gear shop. 
: Turns out it is operational and has 64MB of RAM, a single SCSI disk
: (looks like a second one... once located closest to the front of the
: box... was removed), and it passes all tests except for the
: keyboard/mouse check (I don't have either).

: Last Question:  Is linux available for this machine?  I understand that
: it may be the case that linux does not support turbochannel alphas,
: which from the looks of it the 3000/400 is one.

Correct, the 3000/400 is a TurboChannel machine. Linux doesn't support
the 3000/xxx systems so far. There is a NetBSD port available, but this
doesn't support TurboChannel video options and keyboard/mouse input, so
you're restricted to using a serial console.

If you'd like to start porting Linux to the TurboChannel Alphas, there's
excellent documentation for the complete 3000/xxx series on www.netbsd.com

If someone would give me a TurboChannel Alpha, I'd like to work on a port ...

regards,
	Michael Engel	(engel@unix-ag.uni-siegen.de)




