Do you have the Alpha Firmware CD-ROM? If so, you could load VET from that
onto your system and run an exhaustive exercise test of memory, as well as
much of the rest of the system.

Generally, if the machine has passed POST (Power On Self Test) without
making a noise about memory in testing, you're probably home free.

You are right to ask, as some techniques used for addressing the bits in
the memory chips varies from vendor to vendor and thus there can be
operable difficulties with mixed memory configurations in addition to some
vendors memory not performing at all.

In terms of performance, well, the memory bus speed is fixed, so you have
more memory, but it's not going any faster. You're aggregate workload
capability has improved though. Mor ememory, means more programs or larger
programs all running from memory rather than being paged in and out. Thus,
actual user response times may well be visibly faster.

rtt

