Curt Sampson wrote:
> the person or people with that knowledge. But system administrators,
> programmers and operating system programmers start and leave jobs
> all the time, and the world still goes on.

This is where operating system quality makes a difference. Some
operating systems make it easier for a new sys admin to take over
because of the structure of directories and obvious locations of locally
modified files. Other systems don't "naturally guide" sysadmins to
properly place files and documentation and it is a lot harder to 
take over from a previous sysadmin.

I would rank VMS as a good system in those terms. Logical names are 
an asset that few others systems have. Having to deal with applications
that have file names hardcoded into the binary executables is not 
exactly nice when you've dealt with VMS for so long.

But all this is pointless because beancounters do not understand the
value of this and they are not involved with the pains and tribulations
of a  new sysadmit trying to get control over a system he has just
inherited.



