Mark E. Levy wrote:
> custom financial application.  Applications rarely fit exactly unless
> they're custom.  While there are O/Ss that are specific to certain
> functions, Unix is conseidered "general purpose."  I wouldn't expect a
> company to write their own O/S unless they had some very specific
> needs.  A financial app, for example, would have to be somewhat tailored
> to the way the company does business.


In many cases, financial applications are NOT tailorable. They may be
configurable, but not necessarily tailorable. Examples are SWIFT
interfaces
which have very stringent specifications to interconnect with the
network,
and they get at least 2 upgrades a year. These upgrades MUST be done on
very specific dates. So no bank is stupid enough to start thinkering
with those applications. They prefer to use the documented interfaces to
the app to write side-apps. For instance, their mainframes may generate
SWIFT messages that get sent to the "vanilla" SWIFT application (which
will handle authentication and then send them to SWIFT).

You'd be surprised how many banks run the same software packages and
similar networks. Just because the screens on the ATM look different
doesn't mean that the software at the other end is different.  The
market for these apps is not very big so you end up finding a couple of
suppliers of apps that adhere to standards for each main platform
(IBM-MVS and TANDEM and in a few cases VMS).


Very large organisations such as banks will NOT let employees thinker
with the operating system in any "unsupported" way unless it is PROVEN
to be NECESSARY.
System stability is of primal concern in 7*24 shops and when something
goes wrong, they don't want to wonder about the hacking that was done to
their system (especially if it was done by a previous employee).

Also, those banking apps that banks use are usually "certified" for only
certain versions of operating systems and if the operating system has
been thinkered with, that support would go up in thin air and all finger
pointing would lead to the sys-admin who hacked into the op-system.



