You have yet to understand that not all companies have a need or desire
to hire someone of "your" capabilities.  They want the system to run
without tinkering.  When I buy something, I expect a warranty.  I expect
this warranty to cover any problems I might have, and I expect the
vendor to fix those problems.  What you're saying is that Linux is a
used car, sold as-is, and the owner is expected fix to any problems
which might occur, or hire a mechanic to do it.  That's more involvement
than most customers are interested in.

That doesn't fly, as far as I'm concerned.  If the authors have no
responsibilty to fix problems, how do I know what I'm getting?  The
answer is, I don't.  With a commercial O/S, the vendor has legal
liability to ensure that the product conforms to it's description. 
There is no vendor liability with a freeware O/S, no one to claim
ultimate responsibility.   It's "use at your own risk" and I consider
that risk unacceptable.

Freware O/Ss are great for hobbyists.  They don't rely on them, if they
break, so what?  Fixing them is a great hobby.  But bet my company on
one?  Never.

If your management is stupid enough to accept that risk, fine.  It's not
my problem.

