Must be nice!  I've been foolish enough to work with compilers and assemblers
sold by Borland and Microsoft.  When we found that Microsoft's MS FORTRAN V3.2
was flakey about DATA statements in BLOCK DATAs (sometimes the variables would
be initialized, sometimes not -- I can't believe they didn't test this, what
do they think BLOCK DATAs are *for*?), all we could get out of Microsoft was a
workaround that didn't work.  Luckily we'd already found a slightly better
workaround, meanwhile we switched to Ryan McFarland FORTRAN as soon as we could
(FAR superior product BTW).  Good thing because when MS FORTRAN V4.0 came out,
they'd made an incompatible change in the calling conventions and broke all
our libraries.

Borland is the worst -- I called them up with a list of problems I was having
with TASM V2.01, they told me I was wrong because my bugs weren't on their
list, but if I wanted I could order the current version (V2.02) and see if
things cleared up -- however they would promise nothing, and I would have to
pay full price to get V2.02, and by the way they'd "upgraded" the linker so
that it was slower and no longer worked on pre-386 machines.

I'll take unsupported freeware crap over abuse like this any day of the week,
especially because, whatever the guarantees may say (and they usually say
"no guarantee of fitness for a particular purpose" etc., i.e. they promise
*nothing*), the freeware crap tends to work better in practice than the
expensive "supported" software anyway.

Someone made the point about finger pointing and how having their hands inside
the OS made it easier to prove which software was at fault -- you retorted
by saying how their local mods made the freeware OS even less supportable.
This is not the point!  Even with a bone-stock freeware OS, just having the
ability to see inside the OS when needed so you can catch your expensive
commercial software in the act of screwing up, is a wonderful tool to pin
responsibility on those at fault.  As this person pointed out, many commercial
software houses have the attitude "we'll gladly fix any bugs that are
our fault, providing that the customer invests hundreds of man-hours proving
that the fault is ours, but until then, screw off".

John Wilson
D Bit



