I believe the '@' is the console ODT (Online Debugging Technique?) prompt
from the microprocessor. The single letter commands can be
used to change memory, examine memory, jump to a bootstrap routine etc.
Try typing an (octal) number (e.g. 010) and then typing a forward slash (/).
You should see the contents of that memory location. A linefeed will open
the next sequential location. A <cr> will close the location and return you
to the '@' prompt. 'nnnG' will start executing code at the location given.
When a location is open you can type another number and <lf> or <cr> to
change the contents of that memory location. The value you enter is given in
octal. If you type 1000/ 14747<cr> and then 1000G and then 40/<lf><lf> etc.
you should see 14747 is each of the locations. The 14747 instruction copies
itself to the previous location in memory and then executes itself from the
previous location etc. until it gets to zero. Then the machine traps (takes
an exception) and the behavior varies depending on the exact model processor.

>The PDP-11 was the precurson of the VAX. It was the single longest
>lived line of computers in history, with production running from the end of
>the 1960's to 1996. UNIX was originally developed on them and moved to
>other systems when the 16 bit address space of the PDP-11 became too severe
>a limitation. In 1978, DEC rolled out the VAX as the Virtual Address
>eXtension of the PDP-11 architecture, though the vax doesent bear too much
>similarity to the ancestors inside. DEC aimed for backwards compatibility,
>and based its VAXen on the Unibus and Q-bus, and had a 'PDP-11 compatibility
>mode' that somewhat worked. Anyways, you have a cool machine!
>As for the '@', it looks like a boot prompt fropm a very old revision of unix.
>how old, i cant say, but i know that the '@' was used as early as 1976.
>(way before my time)

