|     It seems that a partitioned cluster is only a danger if one has a 
| resource (disk) which can be independantly accessed by more than one 
| host and it is possible for the hosts to lose communication with each 
| other without also losing connection to thedisk.  This was certainly 
| possible in the CI/Star Coupler/HSC world....    Perhaps I'm being 
| dense here, but it appears to me that this is not a concern in NI 
| based clusters. 

Incorrect.

This issue comes up periodically, and perhaps ought to be mentioned in
the proposed FAQ text (not to mention DEC's documentation).  Cluster
partitioning is an issue whenever there's a shared *resource*.  Disks
are *not* the only kind of resource!  The cluster connection software is
very careful to provide its users with a consistent view of the cluster.
At the base of all these guarantees, all "live" nodes will agree at all
times as to which nodes are part of the cluster.  There are other
guarantees about the order in which cluster messages get delivered. 
Cluster-cognizant software makes use of these guarantees to build
reliable cluster-wide resources.

The cluster disk drivers are the most widely known - and widely used -
piece of cluster-cognizant software.  They take a single physical
resource - a disk - and reliably share it across the cluster.  However,
other pieces of such software exist.  One good example is the shadowing
drivers.  These take multiple physical resources - disk drives perhaps
spread across an Ethernet - and make them look like a single cluster-
wide resource.  In this particular case, the possible failure mode
should be obvious:  In a partitioned cluster, multiple nodes may
independently decide that their local copy of the shadow set is up to
date and modify it.  The result will be an inconsistent, unmergeable
shadow set.

RDB makes similar use of cluster capabilities.  I don't know enough
details to construct an exact failure mode, but I certainly wouldn't
have much confidence that a database constructed on multiple disks that
was modified - on disjoint sets of disks - from partitions of a cluster
would be worth much after the cluster was properly re-formed.

The coordination that keeps the disk drivers, the shadowing software,
and RDB working properly is all based on the same lock manager that user
programs have access to.  Is there user software on your system that is
building a reliable resource abstraction out of disjoint physical
pieces, relying on the lock manager to keep things in sync?  What will
happen to *it* in a partitioned cluster environment?

							-- Jerry



