I guess that we would have to define 'crash'.  I would say that a 'crash'
is an event where all users of a processor lose all work in progress
simultaneously (or very nearly).  I've had to wait out RWASTs a few
times.  Early versions of Pathworks or UCX were fairly regular (3 or 4
times a year) generators.  That's not quite the same as a crash to me.

The post I responded to stated:
    When a new employee arrives here, the mean time to AXP/VMS workstation
    crash after first login is about 2 hours.  This time slowly increases
    over the weeks as the person learns how not to crash the system.

That tells me that there is either an -extremely- poorly written
application
which is running with privs, or that a -brand new- employee with no VMS
experience is given privs.  It is highly unlikely that every new employee
finds a bug in some user mode code which crashes VMS in the first 2
hours.  In an MS product this statement would not be so incredible.



