In article <5uiqus$p4c@r02n01.cac.psu.edu>, Bryan Jensen <bjj+1@arlvax.arl.psu.edu> writes:
> Since my users all go home at night and you guys say VMS is so robust...
> 
> I just executed the commands:
> 
> 	$ mount oda0: dskc
> 	$ create/dir oda0:[test]
> 
> Using AXP/VMS 6.2, DEC OSDS v3.3 (Optical disk software), a DEC RWZ52
> optical disk drive, and a write-locked optical disk.  While not a proper
> thing to do (on account of being write locked), it's quite possible a
> naive user would attempt such a thing.
> 
> I went RWAST.
> 
> Someday I'll read David Cathey's RWAST presentation.
> A reboot was easier though.
> 

I suspect this may be related to the OSDS software rather than VMS.  It
apparently does not emulate a magnetic disk very well.  I currently have a
support call open in which a fairly simple program does nothing more than write
blocks to disk with writevblk and read them back.  It works fine on all
magnetic disks in the customer's cluster and fails on many optical disks.  When
the number of blocks written is near the maximum of 128 for a single write
operation (all of the writexblk function codes have a maximum buffer size of
65536, which is 128 blocks), trash gets written to a random high block (usually
block 127 in my test) instead of the data in the write buffer.  

If you had tried your test on a CDROM instead (using standard VMS drivers), you
would have gotten a nice hard failure:

$ set def MOE$DKA500:[000000]  ! this is a cdrom drive
$ create/dir [test]
%CREATE-E-DIRNOTCRE, [TEST] directory file not created
-SYSTEM-F-WRITLCK, write lock error
$


Wayne
