In article <872708973.28576@dejanews.com>,
 <Guido_Lehwalder@p17.wildcat.fido.de> wrote:
>
>1)
>the station did boot from the ethernet until I did get it,
>so I got no Operating System for this machine :-((
>Where in Germany could I get an free/cheap OS ??
>(Ultrix, (Open)VMS, NetBSD )

No, Yes, No. 
Ultrix is not free.
OpenVMS is avaliable cheap for noncommercial, personal use, 
through DECUS, but i am not sure if the license is offered to
non-US installations. 
NetBSD has not been ported to this machine yet, as far as i know.

>2)
>for which OS do I need a Mouse and how could I get one
>cheap ? the port looks like another form of a 8pin mini-DIN
>like a PS/2-Mouse Port.

You dont *need* a mouse for either ultrix or VMS, the machine
complains at the boot-up tests if it doesent find the proper
electrical conditions on the mouse port. You can fake a mouse by
shorting two of the pins, but i dont rmember which two offhand. 
I think the answer to that is in the microVAX FAQ. 

>
>3)
>what for ram-simms are uses in this machine...are they any
>standard-type ??? it seems to be a 80pin Ram which should
>look like PS/2 Simms...
>on the InterNet I did read that it can be upgraded in pairs.
>either 2*4 or 2*16MB...max. Ram should be 104MB of Ram

They are very NONstandard ECC memory modules from DEC. 
Some wholesalers and dealers of used DEC equipment might still 
carry them. I sold a bunch I had laying aroun about a year ago. 
They do exist but you probably have to ask around a lot. 

>
>4)
>if I did get an OS, how could I install it ?...I didnt got
>a Streamer like a TK50 (often read this name at usenet)...
>I also read that I can use a CDROM which is jumpered to
>512Byte/Block ...is there chance to get the OS on CD
>(Boot-CD for install)....

Your best bet would be, at least initially, to try booting
over the network. If you can get a VMS license, then you could
legally use someone's VMS media (often found on CD) and boot
and install from that. 

>
>Or can I use my HP35470 4mm DAT for installing ?
>My last chance could be my Win-NT Computer with
>Ethernet Card....is there a chance to boot from
>a computer like this as it should been in the past
>when the station was at a company in action ?

Not much of a chance unless you have somehitng that support MOP
on your network, and that generally means having 
another microVAX or DECstation around. 

>
>(Win NT 4.0 Workstation or Server ? or Win95 ? (yes/NO?)

No, No, and No. 

>
>5)
>which OS has TCPIP (I did think all should have it) to
>connect it to my NT Computer....nice would be to use
>the NT Computer HD as storage for the VAXstation.

All OS's released by DEC for this machine suport TCP/IP. 
Does anybody know if the NetBSD people have actulaly ported
it to the VS4000's? 
If NetBSD does exist for this machine, your life will be much easier.
You could try poking around in ftp.netbsd.org and see what there
is to see there.

Assuming you do get the machine running, both VMS and Ultrix
as well as NetBSD can NFS-mount a volume, so you can (assuming you have
an NFS server on your other machine) use its stroage. 

i know i wasnt of too much help, but there isnt a whole lot you can
do with a VS4000 unless a) you can get an openVMS license in germany
form DECUS, or b) there exists unknown to me a NetBSD port to this
machine, or c) you dont care about legal issues and use a copy 
of Ultrix form somewhere. A is reocmmended, B is unsubstantiated hope, and
C is not recommended. That conmpletes the list of operating systems
that that mahcine supports, though, so you're sort of stuck.

good luck!

isildur




