In article <1997Sep3.235954.25568@levels.unisa.edu.au>, system@levels.unisa.edu.au writes:
>$ show net
>OpenVMS Network status for local node  6.47 HOPPER on  3-SEP-1997 23:41:58.75
>
>This is a nonrouting node, and does not have any network information.
>The designated router for HOPPER is node    0 HOPPER.
>
>How do I define the next designated router for our alpha 4000, HOPPER?
>It should be another alpha called BROLGA, decnet number 21.31
>
>We're running OpenVMS 6.2, and the old version of DECnet.

You do not "define" a designated router on a nonrouting node.
You have to run a (area) routing node in the same area as your nonrouting node.
Then (via communication on the net) the nonrouting "knows" the "designated"
(that means the selected - via routing priority and DECnet Address - router
of a possible pool of routers) router.

So it seems, you have three options.

1) Change the DECnet Area of BROLGA to 6 (may impact area 21)
2) Make HOPPER an Area Router for Area 6 (license required)
3) Add an Area Router (CISCO, DECbrouter, DECnis, OpenVMS, ...) for area 6
