In article <5uccht$bng@cynic.portal.ca>, cjs@cynic.portal.ca (Curt Sampson) writes:

> Freely available software software is not free to use in a business
> environment. Any software you use has to be supported in some way,
> and this support always comes at a price.  That price may be in
> the form of paying a lot of money for the software, paying the
> software vendor for a support contract, paying a third party for
> a support contract, or paying in-house staff to do that support.
> Obviously, only the latter two apply to freely available software.
> 
> Now, there are a lot of companies that already use the last option
> above; in fact, the majority of accounting systems I've seen in
> large companies are substantially written in-house and the *only*
> support is through in-house support and development staff. How is
> doing this with accounting software different from doing it with
> operating system software?

The difference is that accounting packages do not have other software
whose machine operation depends on them.  Thus there is little finger
pointing from other applications saying that their misbehaviour is due
to the locally maintained software (presuming the whole shebang is on
an operating system with memory protection, resource quotas, etc.

Presume a perfect Linux system and a commercial application on top of
it which has a defect.  What are the chances the commercial vendor will
try to point the finger at the OS (just how many local changes are on
this machine where our software has difficulty?).

Vendors of software running on VMS are reasonably certain they can
duplicate the exact OS version in-house to analyze problem reports.
The same goes for other operating systems from organizations large
enough to not be able to issue new releases too quickly.

Larry Kilgallen



