In article <Pine.SUN.3.90.970829164839.12224A-100000@spof01.gsfc.nasa.gov>, Chris Raymond <raymond@spof01.gsfc.nasa.gov> writes:

  Somewhat reordered...  And assuming this isn't just trolling...

:My boss has proposed that a link from our facility to another facility
:which is VAX-heavy be changed.  Currently, this link uses DECnet protocol
:and travels over 10-Base-T Ethernet.  M<y boss has proposed we change
:the protocol used to IP.
..
:My reaction is:  HUH?  

  My reaction to your boss's request is also: HUH?

  This appears to be a typical wrench that non-technical folk will
  tend to throw into a working network configuration.  This particular
  wrench can be simplied into the belief statement: "IP good.  DECnet
  bad."  Ask yourself -- and your boss -- does what you have do what
  you need in a fashion you can afford?  And does what you are replacing
  your existing environment with provide you with something better?  And,
  if you are going to migrate, can the environments coexist?

  In practice, there are likely already a large number of protocols
  running over the Ethernet -- both IP and DECnet can and regularly
  do coexist on the same Ethernet network, as does LAT, SCS datagrams,
  bridge multicasts, and other messages.  In other words, the Ethernet
  link is likely already running IP.  (There, wasn't that easy? :-)

  Though -- if you really want to have fun :-) -- DECnet-Plus can
  operate over an IP transport, in addition to operating over the
  more traditional DDCMP and Ethernet transports.

:In the course of a very heated discussion the other day (the people at
:the other facility were intent on shooting down the idea), one of the
:people at the other facility made the remark, "DECnet is a secure
:protocol;  IP is not."

  There are comparatively few crackers that have directly attempted
  attacks against the DECnet protocols, while there are various IP
  protocol-level attacks around, and some of these protocol attacks
  have seen widespread use. 

  There are various security attributes -- one frustration I've seen
  more than a few times involves the source username -- that do not
  exist in a secure fashion in the IP protocol suite.  This is how
  DECnet can audit the remote accessor, and how proxies can be made
  to work on a per-user basis, and how constructs like the automatic
  login facility (ALF) are implemented.

  Conversely, there are mechanisms -- such as Kerberos -- that are
  typically available only in the IP space. 

  In other words, it is a tradeoff -- I use both DECnet- and IP-based
  protocols quite regularly...

  But given that part of the design of the Ethernet involved support
  for multiple protocols, this -- without more information -- looks
  like classic "Dilbert" material.  Use both.  You probably already
  are using both...

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
