> >Tim Shoppa <shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca> wrote:
> >>I based the above statement on my 1971 "PDP-11/20, /15, /R20"
> >>processor handbook.  Yet a much later book "PDP-11 Unibus Processor
> >>Handbook: 11/84, /44, and /24" says that the only -11/03 and Falcon
> >>lack 4-level interrupts.  So now I'm confused...

...

> On p. 4 of my "PDP-11/20, /15, /R20" handbook it says:
> 
>   The PDP-11/15 has only a single line of interrupt (any number of
>   devices).  A multi-line system, like that of the PDP-11/20, is
>   optional on the PDP-11/15 (KF11-A).
> 
> Now it's very possible that just about every PDP-11/15 (KC11) 
> shipped with the optional KF11-A.  This happens in other boxes:
> for example, the MMU was listed as optional on PDP-11/23+'s, but
> I've never seen a /23+ without the MMU installed.

The original KA-11 had a few offspring. The was the 11/15, aka KC-11,
in a full size BA-11? and the ***11/10***, a KA-11 in a half length
BA-11?, just like the OEM PDP-8 of the same vintage.

The KC-11 could be converted to a KA-11 by adding a KH-11 `large
system option'. I'd always understood this to be a DMA arbiter,
but it could have been to enable 4 interupt lines.

Remember, the 11/45, the KB-11 was the second 11 off the rank, and
predated the 40/35. The KB included supervisor mode, second set
of GPRs 0-5, I/D space and optionally, solid state memory.

~Paul




