Hi,

We are running MS-SQL Server 6.5 on a DEC NT Cluster (SP 1-1)
and have been having trouble with the database failback functionality
under certain circumstances.

It seems that when we put a database on more than one
device (e.g. one file for log and one file for data segments),
then the database ends up being unusable after a failback.

If the database is placed only upon one logical device (i.e. log
and data together in one file), then every works fine.

It seems that the fallback stored procedures, which write info from
the spt_fallback tables into sysusages, sysdevices and sysdatabases
have a hard time when there is more than one device per database.
If you check out the size of the DB devices with Enterprise Manager,
they show strange numbers (often negative) (i.e. a possible programming
error). 
Also, you can't usually stop and restart the SQL Server without booting
... although this usually happens after trying a dump.

We would really like to put the log and data on separate devices
(even though we are running RAID-5), but it seems like a no-go at the
moment.

Has anybody else had similar problems or have any ideas ?

/Russell



