In article <vTPaEtSUyrgL@malvm1.mala.bc.ca>, dunnett@mala.bc.ca (Malcolm Dunnett) writes:
> This is just a special case of a node which serves disks to the cluster
> leaving and re-entering the cluster at a later time (before MVTIMEOUT).
> While it's probable that the disks haven't been updated in isolation between

Actually, I would say that if it is the system disk of the other node,
the opposite is true: it has certainly been updated during the time when
that node was its own cluster.

> the crash and the reboot there's no guarantee of this, so VMS has to be designed to
> handle the general case (ie assume nothing about the state of disks
> on a new cluster member).

[...]

>    I believe the mechanism VMS uses is to record the "date and time of
> last mount" in the SCB. If the disk has been mounted while not served

If it's anything like volume shadowing, it records the quadword time but
has a mechanism to guarantee that the value always moves forward and never
backwards - so it's really a pseudo-time.  The other cluster members need
not have precisely synchronized internal clocks but would record the current
contents of the quadword as a magic check value.

> to the cluster it will have a different date/time here than the
> cluster has for the disk. When the node re-enters the cluster the
> difference in these values will be noted and the mount verify won't
> complete. 

Makes sense.  Now in how many other areas in VMS or in various cluster
aware layered products, databases, etc., does something equivalent have
to be done, and have they done it right?

Remember that it could be considered a perfectly reasonable assumption that
as long as you hold an exclusive clusterwide lock that protects a file
which you periodically open, update, and close, it will never be touched
in the interim by someone else.  A database server might work this way,
for example, if it were designed to minimize the number of channels open
at a time, and if it did this it might not detect a situation in which
a drive was dismounted and remounted to recover from a mount verification
failure such as the kind we have been discussing.

Again, it takes some tortured logic to come up with a scenario in which
corruption occurs, and this is probably because the mount verification
mechanism does a good job of protecting the disk volumes, which are
the only places we would normally expect to have anything change in a
way that could affect cluster integrity when a lost node rejoined.

As you say, setting VAXCLUSTER=0 and rebooting would result in similar
exposures in any event, and since many of us have done this in the
past with no ill effect and are not enjoined by Digital against doing
so, we tend to assume that there are no major risks associated with it.

This leaves cluster communications.  I previously mentioned I would
expect a CLUEXIT if communications between two halves of a partitioned
cluster were restored, but are all the bases covered for the case where
only a random packet from one instance of the cluster makes it through
and is seen by a node of the other?  I suppose they could be, since,
after all, even an SCS broadcast packet most likely includes the ID of
the transmitting node, and the remaining nodes should reject it if it
is not from a current member of their cluster.

So, who knows?  If you don't have any critical shared resources outside
of properly mounted VMS volumes, and do not access shared files via sneak
paths like NFS serving, i.e., paths which in some network failure modes
would remain up even if cluster communications were down, it may in fact
be relatively safe to have a partitioned NI-only cluster.

--
