George Shouse wrote:
> This amzaes me.  Even running in processes with full priveleges, I find
> it hard to crash VMS unless I am in kernel or at least exec.  I don't
> generally turn on priveleges unless I need them.  What are they doing
> that crashes VMS?  We are talking about VMS being gone and only
> a reboot being able to fix it, right?

How about the good old MUA0: tape drive whose server process goes RWAST
and only a reboot safely makes this device available again ?

How about an application which has a server process that runs as an ACP
and cannot be stopped properly (goes RWAST) when it goes nuts ?

Yes, I know that are a few utilities that make it possible to unlock
certain RWAST processes, but they are perhaps not worthy of 7*24 shops
who can use them only to postone a reboot.

Yes, you can argue that *VMS* didn't crash. But if you need to reboot it
to unlock a (poorly written) application the end result is the same.

The quality of an application is much more important than the quality of
the operating system. However, I do admit that it is much easier to
write
robust applications on VMS than it is to write them on DOS or NT.



