In article <5ufreg$tcb$1@news.wizvax.net>, John Wilson <wilson@dbit.com> wrote:
>In article <5ufija$6vt$1@nntp.ucs.ubc.ca>,
>Tim Shoppa <shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca> wrote:
>>I based the above statement on my 1971 "PDP-11/20, /15, /R20"
>>processor handbook.  Yet a much later book "PDP-11 Unibus Processor
>>Handbook: 11/84, /44, and /24" says that the only -11/03 and Falcon
>>lack 4-level interrupts.  So now I'm confused...
>
>The chart in that book appears to be yet another incarnation of the
>"PDP-11 Family Differences Table" that appears in all later PDP-11
>processor docs.  FWIW, in working on E11 I've found that this table
>contains enough errors to be pretty much worthless, so I'd be more
>inclined to believe the older docs.

Yes, those "Family Differences Tables" tend to have quite a few
mistakes.  I think the version printed in the "11/84, /44, /24" is
the latest table I have, and it tends to have corrected many of the
mistakes that crept into the ones that came a few years before,
but it still has a number of obvious typos.

On p. 4 of my "PDP-11/20, /15, /R20" handbook it says:

  The PDP-11/15 has only a single line of interrupt (any number of
  devices).  A multi-line system, like that of the PDP-11/20, is
  optional on the PDP-11/15 (KF11-A).

Now it's very possible that just about every PDP-11/15 (KC11) 
shipped with the optional KF11-A.  This happens in other boxes:
for example, the MMU was listed as optional on PDP-11/23+'s, but
I've never seen a /23+ without the MMU installed.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



