In article <5u80cn$1e60@r02n01.cac.psu.edu>, Bryan Jensen <bjj+1@arlvax.arl.psu.edu> writes:
>
>When a new employee arrives here, the mean time to AXP/VMS workstation
>crash after first login is about 2 hours.  This time slowly increases
>over the weeks as the person learns how not to crash the system.

This indicates a system failure - not of the OS, but of your site's
management methods.  If you have "N" employees, each of whom knows how to
"not crash the system", then why on earth don't you have at least 1 of them
document this information and provide it in clearly written form to the new
employees? 

That being said, I see from other posts that you use Open3D.  Well, Open3D/
OpenGL can be an evil beast.  When I ported Xforms to OpenVMS and did the 
OpenGL pieces I didn't have OpenGL extensions on my OpenVMS X11 server. (Is
that open enough for everybody?)  Anyway, I set the display to an SGI in
another room, and it crashed the X11 server on the SGI when I ran the OpenGL
test program.  It was just like pulling the switch on the monitor - boom, 
blank screen.  There was nothing wrong with Xforms, it was a lethal
incompatibility at the OpenGL level, sending the same display to a Digital
Unix system worked much better.   (It would not surprise me much if similar
incompatibilities existed between the server and client pieces of Open3D 
itself.)  That version of Open3D was almost 3 years old, and they've 
revised it a bit since then.  So, if you must use Open3D, at the very least
I'd be sure to have the latest and greatest revision on every single
machine. 

Regards,

