Message-ID: <01bcb7c1$f88d1d20$51bd699f@hawthorn.dlc.state.vt.us>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 269554125
Organization: Vermont Department of Liquor Control
Newsgroups: alt.unix.wizards.free,comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.questions


The DECserver 90M with which my Digital Alpha Unix system is linked to a
set of modems, is working fine with one exception.  Whenever it
initializes, whether due to a power outage or the INITIALIZE command, it
goes through the startup process just fine, loads from Flash RAM
successfully, and displays status messages on a console terminal on the
console port indicating all of this.  Then just when it's displayed the
last message and should be ready to start working, instead, it freezes up. 
The eight LEDs for the eight ports start to blink: 5-8, then 1-4, then 5-8,
and back and forth.  This goes on for five solid minutes during which the
90M won't respond to keystrokes on the console, won't react to attempts to
telnet to it, won't respond when a LAT driver tries to go through it, and
is generally uncommunicative.  Then when the five minutes is up, the lights
all clear and the 90M starts working just fine.

None of the (very very limited) manuals I have suggest this as a diagnostic
warning that means anything.  DEC tech support was unable to guess what it
might mean.  It's not a problem with the loading from Flash RAM because
that's already completed before this starts, and because everything works
fine once this is done.  I've been able to find no references to this
anywhere on the Net or anywhere else, for that matter.

What I'd like to see is if this didn't do this at all, as it's possible one
of our polling programs could get hung up trying to talk to the modem if a
power blip happens to cause a reset at the wrong moment (we have the 90M on
a UPS but you can't be too certain).

Anyone have any idea what is going on, how I can stop it, or at least an
idea of where I could go to find out?  I'm desperate; *ANY* help would be
vastly appreciated.

