In comp.sys.dec Frank J. Perricone <frank@dlc.state.vt.us> wrote:
> Anyone have any idea what is going on, how I can stop it, or at least an
> idea of where I could go to find out?  I'm desperate; *ANY* help would be
> vastly appreciated.

  [All of the attached assumes the DS90M is one of the DS9x products that
uses DNAS - DECserver Network Access Software. Some DS9x products run older
code that's pretty brain-dead.]

  After it starts working, log in and do a "SHOW SERVER STATUS" and look at
the "Selftest Status" and "Software Status" fields. Anything other than "Nor-
mal" might provide a clue.

  I've seen this sort of thing when trying to boot newer code on older boxes.
It comes from 2 different problems:

  1) prior corruption of then-unused config memory causes a release that uses
     that config memory location to be confused. Usually the only way to fix
     this is to reset the server to factory defaults (usually a button held in
     during power-up). Of course, you lose all your settings, so save 'em first
     before trying this.

  2) brain-dead defaults in new code. As an example, DNAS 2.2 sends pretty-much
     continuous DHCP requests unless you do "DEFINE INTERNET DHCP DISABLED" and
     reboot the server. Likewise, older versions of DNAS try to BOOTP their IP
     address and mask if not set - if you're not using IP on the box, do a "DEF
     INTER DISA" and reboot. If you are using IP, make sure the IP address and
     netmask are properly set.

